I'm having problems compiling an application requiring 32-bit pointers
(-xtaso_short) and using stdarg.h (ansi-style varargs) on ALPHAstations
running both DU 3.2 and 4.0B (they both seem to give similar
complaints).

The problem is that the (ansi, -std1) C compiler complains about: 

	#include <stdarg.h>

	void f ( char *opt, ... )
	{  va_list args;

		:
	   va_start(args,opt);	/* <=== this is line XXX */
		:
	}

When compiled using "cc -std1 -xtaso_short -c banana.c" I get the
following message:

cc: Error: banana.c, line XXX: In this statement, the size of "args" is
less than the size of a va_list structure as defined in stdarg.h and
varargs.h.

Is there more I should know? The source compiles (and runs) fine in
64-bit mode (without the option -xtaso_short). The application is sadly
pointer-rich Ram-poor so I REALLY want to use 32-bit pointers if at all
possible.

-- jim

