In article <6i898t$67l@bolivia.earthlink.net>, 
    	"Juergen Schreck" <juesch@earthlink.net> writes:

> To increase backbone bandwith, I suggested that they install a couple more
> NIC's in the server and segment their network. They told that per their
> knowledge there could only be one TCP/IP adapter in a VMS system? Is that
> true?

        Absolutely _untrue_.  A have two  AS4100's  each  of which has 3
    FDDI plus 3 Ethernet adapters.  All _five_ adapters on both machines
    are  configured  to  run TCP/IP (under Multinet).  Of  course,  each
    interface connects to a separate subnet, but you knew that...   It's
    only  a  question  of  how  many  PCI slots you have available.  The
    AS4000 can have up to 16  slots  IIRC (the AS4100's are limited to 8
    slots).
 
> If so, another option would be to replace the 100Mbit NIC with a Gigabit
> Ethernet NIC and adjust the network switching accordingly. Is GB-ENET NIC 
> available for the 4000? and does the VMS support it?

        I've seen no announcement  of  VMS support for Gigabit Ethernet,
    nor any support adapters for same.  You'd probably have to wait for
    at least VMS 7.2 (rumored to be released in late Fall).

> Secondly, can the DEC RAID configuration be adjusted to a RAID Level 3? Or
> would it be easier to use external RAID's for that? What kind of SCSI-BUS
> expansion is possible with the 4000? Are there working FibreChannel adapters
> yet?

        About RAID, etc., you'll need  to  tell us more about the system
    configuration.    Are  you  use  HSZ  controllers  in   StorageWorks
    cabinets, or a PCI-based RAID adapter?  The HSZ's  do  RAID  3/5  (I
    think   they  call  it).   Ultra-SCSI  adapters  are  available  and
    supported under VMS (may require V7.1-1H1).  FibreChannel is coming,
    probably in the same time frame  as  VMS 7.2, but it's not here yet.
    Changes  are  that  wouldn't  buy you anything  anyway,  since  your
    bottleneck appears to be in the network, not in storage access  from
    the AS4000 (unless you can tell us otherwise).

> Also, the SGI's are connected via NFS2 to the DEC. I'm measuring
> transfer-speeds at only 0.5 MB/s on 10MBit clients and only about 0.85 MB/s
> on 100Mbit clients. Is this a poor NFS implementation on the DEC?

        Very unlikely it's  the  NFS  inplementation  (BTW, which TCP/IP
    stack  is being used on the Alpha?).  Look for high collision  rates
    on the network.

        As a point of reference, we're backing up Sun UltraSparcs to VMS
    storage (so we get _good_ VMS  backups  of  the Sun file systems) by
    doing  file  system  dumps  on the Suns to VMS disks  that  are  NFS
    mounted (Multinet for TCP/IP, storage is over the  CI  to  HSJ52's).
    It  turns  out  the  Suns  have  interfaces to two of the 5 networks
    mentioned above.  When we did initial tests, the Suns were accessing
    the  NFS  volumes  on  our  LAVC  network.   One  Sun  could  easily
    _saturate_ the LAVC leading to significant, noticable affects on our
    satellite  VAXstations!  I don't know if the Suns were violating the
    protocol rules and not backing-off  appropriately  in  the  face  of
    substantial  collision  rates,  but  we  saw  _very_  high  Ethernet
    utilization rates.  We then changed  the  Sun  to NFS-mount over the
    other  network, to which the Alpha has an FDDI connetion and the Sun
    has  10-base-T  (10-Mbit  Ethernet)  connections.   In  this   mode,
    everyone  is  happy and the Alpha has no problem keeping up with the
    Sun.

        The point is, the  network  _is_  the  bottleneck.   You need to
    solve that before doing anything else.

> Again, I truly appreciate any input. If possible, please cc: my email
> address with your response. It is juesch@earthlink.net.

        Posted and mailed.

            -Ken
