Organization: I'm just a guy with a computer, that's my story anyway
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Used to be that if you created a cloneset in ADVfs, then went off and
updated a lot of
data in the original fileset while the clone still existed, you could fill
up the physical storage
if there was less than 50% of it free to begin with, when this happened a
write() to a previously
uncloned block would fail with E_NOSPACE errors. To most applications this
was a bad thing
since they weren't  coded to deal well with a NOSPACE error when updating a
pre-existing disk
block so they tended to react poorly (I'm thinking specifically of Oracle,
which would crash in new
and interesting ways when this occurred).

I was told today that this behavior has changed in the current version, now
the response to
the first problematic write() that occurs after the physical media fills up
is to delete the cloneset, thus
eliminating the need to copy the block in question (and incidentally freeing
up the space too), and
proceed with the write(), whatever was reading from the cloneset (frequently
a backup job) would
of course die but at least the application doing r/w access would putter
along happily not knowing
anything had ever happened, a much better approach all in all.

Can anyone confirm if this is true and which version of ADVfs this appeared
in?

Thanks
