In article <6i8msc$8m0$1@ocean.cup.hp.com>, foo@bar.baz (Rick Jones) writes:
>Jonathan Stone (jonathan@Cup.DSG.Stanford.EDU) wrote:
>: Huh? Maybe that's true on vaxes, but not on current-generation
>: machines with good software.
>
>If one was writing NFSv2 to a server without write acceleration, I
>suspect that 100BT and 10BT performance would be about the same. Now,
>you might say that such a system does not have "good" software.
>
>Also, if one has a latency sensitive application, I would not be
>surprised if it ran at about the same speed as before. Especially if
>the packets it sent back and forth were on the smallish side.
> 
>rick jones

Well, some time ago I've got good results with DECNET(task-to-task) vs TCP/IP.
For example the transfer rate between two Alpha 3000-500's on FDDI
are <1MB/s for TCP and ~5MB/s for DECNET.

The transfer rate over thin Ethernet(via bridge though) was ~300KB/s
for TCP/IP and 800KB/s for DECNET, and about 700-800KB/s for
DECNET running on IBM/PC 486 with DOS 6.2 :)

The good old VAX-11/780 managed to get 240KB/s for DECNET (has 4MBit/s card)
with low(25% CPU utilization, mostly interrupt), 
while the TCP/IP (UCX) gave 160KB/s with 100% CPU :(

If I remember correctly there was a paper from DEC WRL(or SRL...)
in '88 about "myths" of low speed of Ethernet, and another paper
about some "tweaks" in DEC OSF/1 TCP/IP stack to get 100Mbits/s over FDDI...
But it seems that their tweaks are not in production code.

Did anybody have the "official" benchmarks results of DEC TCP/IP stack on
VMS and DUNIX ?

----
Oleg




