In article <her-ya02408000R2804982148220001@enews.newsguy.com>, her@mail.nospam (Concerned Customer) writes:
>I see where this is finally available for the Macintosh but I have read
>many reports where DEC's emulation will take care of Intel applications as
>well as Word and Excel being available for ALPHA as native applications. I
>have also read statements where Excel and Word have been available for
>ALPHA for some time now.

Right.  However, if you go to 

  http://www.asia-pacific.digital.com/semiconductor/amt/index.html

and read up on FX!32, you will see that it has good support for Office 97, 
but I don't see anything in there about Office 98.  The pisser is that, 
even though Excel and Word are available native, you can't mix the native
versions with the Intel Office.  I expect that's probably true for Office
98 as well.  For more Office 97 info, see:

http://www.asia-pacific.digital.com/semiconductor/amt/fx32/fx-readme.htm#473888

All that being said, I have not tried Office 97.  I did try Lotus Smart 
Suite 97, and had no problems with it.  On an AlphaStation 200 4/166 with
64 Mb of memory, after optimization, it (and all other software) runs at 
about 2/3 the speed of a 90 Mhz Pentium (a reference PC I have sitting right 
next to it,) so let's say about like a 60 Mhz Pentium.  If things scale
well, then software in FX!32 on a 500 Mhz Alpha would be roughly equivalent
to a 180 Mhz Pentium. 

Oh yes, it takes *forever* to load software.  I think this is because FX!32
doesn't get a chance to optimize the installer program.  Also, you 
definitely want to schedule an immediate optimization for anything you 
really plan on using.

Bottom line, on a 500 Mhz Alpha you'll probably be satisified with the 
speed of most applications running in FX!32.  They won't be nearly as fast 
as native, but they will be good enough.  However, on the older machines, 
like our 4/166, things are usable, but just slow enough to be annoying.
Also, be aware that FX!32 will not do drivers, and you don't have to look 
as far as you might think to run into a driver.  For instance, I tried to
run the LaserGraphics software (for driving their slide printers) on WNT/
Alpha.  The program part worked, but the driver wouldn't, so there was no 
way for the program to talk to the slide printer, or to print to BLL files
for transfer to another machine for actual rendering.  Reason, both output 
paths were through a driver.  Ie, WNT/Alpha was worthless for the intended
application. 

Its the same old story - it doesn't matter how many applications are on a 
platform if the one you need isn't.

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



