In article <6i9r3k$goq@flonk.uk.sun.com>, Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com#> writes:

> It may help you to understand that Sun owns
> the patents for Reflective Memory. And guess
> what Digital use reflective memory for.

Reflective Memory is the sole cluster interconnect for Digital Unix Clusters.

By way of contrast, VMS Clusters can use any _combination_ of:

	CI
	DSSI
	Ethernet
	FDDI
	Reflective Memory
	SCSI

and maybe even ATM by now, but I don't really track it.

The interesting thing is that being restricted to a single interconnect
Digital Unix is able to remain competitive in the Unix "cluster" arena,
(as proven by Mr. Harrison's repeated attempts to classify all Digital
Unix approaches as being cluster-centric).  To me, that indicates the
level of immaturity in all flavors of Unix "clustering".

To learn more about the innards of VMS, please check out the 800 page
VMS Internals and Data Structures book from your corporate library.  Do
come up to speed quickly before Rob Young secures copyright permission
to type the whole thing in as one of his newsgroup responses :-).

