> There's this little invention called a switch that should solve this
> problem. Segment the network with a switch between your hubs (or do
> without hubs entirely, in the best case, high dollar usage, solution).
--

Geez, I was gonna say that myself.

We run 3 clusters of 16 alphas each, using Bay 28115 switches.
The alphas each have DE500 fast ethernet cards.

We regularlly get 8-10 MB/sec from the network, even for file I/O.
This is for large transfers. E.g., I just did a file copy over fast
ethernet and got the following..

[foobar-83] ll gcc-2.8.1.tar.gz 
-rw-rw-r--   1 grunwald abelian  8447495 Mar  4 05:16 gcc-2.8.1.tar.gz
[foobar-84] time cp gcc-2.8.1.tar.gz /dev/null
0.007u 0.387s 0:01.93 19.6% 0+1k 1032+2io 1pf+0w


That's 4.3MB/second using NFS, going through a switch and then a
router.

As someone else pointed out, FE doesn't help latency -- you still need
to hit the full TCP/IP stack and the PCI bus latency doesn't get any
smaller. But your bandwidth should go way up.


-- 
