In article <35487AF7.41C6@radia1.com>, Jim Tuccillo  <jjt@radia1.com> wrote:
>
>The original table ( below ) is not valid since it compares SGI local
>node latency ( and not the latency to any memory location in the system
>) to the latency for any memory location for the DEC, SUN, and HP
>uniform memory access SMPs.

Of course it is a "valid" comparison, because I clearly stated
what the assumptions/restrictions were.

In many cases, when running N independent processes on an N cpu
system, you will find almost no remote memory references -- most
of the jobs have working sets that are smaller than the node's
memory, or they use the caches effectively and don't make many
memory references to *any* locations.  The SPEC_rate benchmarking
methodology has the first characteristic for all the tests, and
the second characteristic for more than 1/2 of the 18 benchmarks.

Obviously, it is quite possible for one job to use all the memory
in the machine.  If the accesses to that global memory are uniformly
distributed, then you will see the average latency that I posted
in my follow-up analysis.  Whether this is the right way for a
particular user to view the machine depends on what that user's
code is doing....



>It is like comparing apples to bananas. It
>makes more sense to compare the average latency in the above table to
>the DEC, HP, and SUN numbers below. 

Only if you are doing random accesses across the whole memory of
the machine.  This is a valid, but rare, access pattern.   You
won't see it when running multiple independent jobs, you won't
see it when running most parallelized scientific/engineering
applications.  You will see these uniform, global access patterns
(as Ashok Singal pointed out) for TPC-C benchmarks (but less so
for TPC-D), and for certain codes run by the National Security
Agency.
