In article <3548EC1B.41C6@radia1.com>, Jim Tuccillo  <jjt@radia1.com> wrote:
>John McCalpin wrote:
>> 
>> In article <35487AF7.41C6@radia1.com>, Jim Tuccillo  <jjt@radia1.com> wrote:
>> >
>> >The original table ( below ) is not valid since it compares SGI local
>> >node latency ( and not the latency to any memory location in the system
>> >) to the latency for any memory location for the DEC, SUN, and HP
>> >uniform memory access SMPs.
>> 
>> Of course it is a "valid" comparison, because I clearly stated
>> what the assumptions/restrictions were.
>
>Nonsense. You were trying to make the point that SGI equipment doesnt
>suffer the decrease in memory performance of other systems when the
>number of CPUs is increased. 

Precisely.  There is no penalty in local memory latency as the size
of the machine is increased.  Of course the worse case remote accesses
get slower as the machine size grows -- that is simple physics.  And,
of course, the average gets worse as the machine size grows -- that is
simple physics, too.  But in the Origin2000, neither the worst case 
nor the average case effects the local memory latency.  This statement
can *not* be made of the other systems.

So what I am saying is that the "uniform" access machines tend to be 
uniformly *high* latency as the machine size grows.  There is no
local, reduced-latency memory in these "flat" machines that one could
take advantage of to improve performance.  The Origin provides this,
and does it in a way that does not break the SMP programming model.
