Scott Walters <scott@wopr.iuinc.com> wrote:
}On the network side, I haven't seen any commercial/corporate apps ...
}that really make the latency vs. bandwidth thing an issue.
   [well known uses for bigbandwidth]
}When things choke it is because the pipe is too small, not cause
}sessions aren't snappy enough.

}Bandwidth is getting *real* cheap.  Especially for LAN's.

   Latency will always be expensive for WANs (until we get subspace radio :)

   Latency scales with distance -- an app that works perfectly
   fine on a LAN may well choke on a WAN with *more* bandwidth.

   Not so long ago I was asked to see why an application was
   performing so abysmally in its first field test.  An operation
   that took seconds where it was developed ("works fine for us") [tm]
   was taking the better part of an hour!

   Turns out the client program was issuing a whole series of (small)
   queries to the DB on the server.  This reply-respond-repeat isn't
   very visible when the client and server are a few feet of ethernet
   apart -- when they have more than 1000 miles of T3 between them,
   you have a lot of dead air time.

   This failure to consider latency when architecting this app,
   pretty much left the project a total loss.

John
