[...]
> So if I asked the reseller to quote for an AS1000A with 16 user vms license
> they should have included the price of the base vms license in the original
> quote.

        Yes.  And it  is  _their_  responsibility  to  get  you the base
    license.   Whether  this is a new or refurbished system, _they_  are
    supposed to get the details to DEC and then DEC  issues  the  actual
    PAK.

> Should a second user AS1000A always have a base license?

        I'm not sure  I  understand  what  you  mean  by, "a second user
    AS1000A".  If it's a separate system, yes, it needs its own base VMS
    license.   If  you're  talking about loading another,  e.g.,  2-user
    VMS-USER license on the same system that has  the  16-user  VMS-USER
    license, then no.  You need just one VMS base license per system.

>                                                          I recall a
> transfer fee being mentioned by the reseller, would this be for
> transferring the base license from the previous owner?

        For a refurbished/used (or,  as  the  used  car salesmen like to
    say,  "previously owned" :-) system, the base VMS license stays with
    the system.  It should be  transferred  to  the  new  owner  by  the
    previous  owner.   Digital  does  this  for  a nominal fee of US$300
    (about)  per  _system_  and  allows  you  to  transfer  all  "System
    Integrated  Product" licenses, such as VMScluster, DECnet, etc.,  in
    addition to (base) VMS, for  the  one  fee.   Layered  products  are
    excluded.   I  don't  happen  to  know whether VMS-USER licenses are
    transferable...

        In any case, if a reseller  sells  you a system "with VMS", that
    price  _should_ include any transfer fees since you can't run VMS on
    the system until you have the PAKs, which must be  transferred.   If
    not, I wouldn't pay!  (Well, in principle, anyway...)

