In article <casper.893788786@uk-usenet.uk.sun.com>, Casper.Dik@Holland.Sun.Com (Casper H.S. Dik - Network Security Engineer) writes:

> I still don't so how Galaxies are something other than a combination of the
> following:
> 	- a scalability band-aid
> 	  [ running multple instances of an OS is one way to get around
> 	    software bottlenecks; but no way of getting past hardware
> 	    bottlenecks ]

In some sense multiprocessing itself is a performance band-aid, for those
who cannot build a single CPU which is fast enough.  Sun does this today,
DEC did it ten years ago when they could not build a VAX faster than the
CVAX.  DEC does this today.  Cray does this.

Likewise, wider busses are a performance band-aid, for use only by those
who cannot build a bus which is fast enough.  Who are those vendors ?
Well, anyone who uses a bus width greater than 8 bits, because "fast
enough" to most vendors means "much better than the competition".

So yes, the Galaxy would seem to be yet another way to attack software
bottlenecks, and hardware bottlenecks must be dealt with by hardware.

Of course the line between software and hardware bottlenecks can be
fuzzy at times.  If my software wants to read a single byte from disk
and does so by reading in an entire file, that would work if the file
and the hardware had certain characteristics and would perform poorly
for other sets of characteristics.  At the extremes (1 GB file from
which one byte is needed, 10 minutes rotational latency), it is easy
to say whether the bottleneck is in software or hardware.  In the
middle ground, however, there can be cases where _either_ hardware
or software can be modified to advantage.

But as you say, Galaxy is a software entity, and DEC also needs to
support it with hardware.  Perhaps they are reading this thread and
will consider hardware innovations :-)

> 	- main-frame style resource control
> 	  [ you dedicate a number of CPUS and certain parts of the memory
> 	    to a specific OS instance as resource control ]

"Resource control" is one way to describe it.  "Contention avoidance"
is another.  Again based on figures posted here regarding large
systems from other vendors, adding CPU number 43 gives you nowhere
near the bang-for-the-buck that CPU number 2 did.  The Galaxy aim
of "contention avoidance" at the OS level is aimed at that law of
diminishing returns.

Of course the other aspect of moving resources between OS instances is
the flexibility of REallocating resources on the fly.  Considering the
fuss about "hot-swap" disk drives, such flexibility would seem to be
a goal of those who run programs other than benchmarks.

> But perhaps Im all mistaken and someone can explain how software
> can make hardware perform better than its specifications.

I think what OS folk can do is make hardware perform _up_to_ its
specifications.  Consider the raw transfer rates of disk drives.
How often is the whole system running to fill that transfer bandwidth ?
I bet the folks reading this thread could write a program to make their
favorite hardware get quite close to its potential, but how many actual
user applications do that?  (And how maintainable is the resulting program).

And if your OS department has achieved perfection in this area,
it is time to worry about what your hardware department is going
to do next, because if they ever build a machine faster than the
fastest you have now, a new set of bottlenecks will appear which
were invisible with the old hardware.

Larry Kilgallen



