> I can't explain that. Maybe someone from Sun can explain how Dynamic
> System Domains make StarFire the greatest thing since sliced bread?

Don't need to just ask a StarFire Customer 70% use domains. Butseriously Dynamic
Domains give them the same sort of functionallity
as IBM LPARS and most of them want this sort of capability on
a large server that is going to support multiple applications.

The benefits are:

Different times can be set for indevidual domains (now how
would that be usefull.

Different OS releases or different patch levels of the same
OS can be run on each domain.

Apps from one domain cannot steal resources from
another domain.

But CPU's and Memory can be moved from domain
to domain dynamically.

An example of this:

One customer I work for has a dual domain Starfire
one domain is runs a high volume OLTP application
and one runs a DSS database. For various good
reasons they cannot run on one very large system.

This customer can do his batch processing in the
DSS domain at night, then move CPU's and memory
into the OLTP domain before the daily OLTP work
load increases.

This is a simple configuration which would require
two much larger separate systems to do the job
without domains.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect






