John McCalpin (mccalpin@frakir.engr.sgi.com) wrote:
: In article <6hpadq$le2$1@alanya.m.isar.de>,
: Lupe Christoph <lupe@alanya.m.isar.de> wrote:
: >
: >If you could quote a figure how "putting the memory the other side of
: >a bus" increased latency, I could start arguing about that. 

: There is a lot of suggestive data out there, if you know how
: to look at it....

: Several vendors offer large SMP machines using the same cpus as
: in smaller machines (uniprocessors or small SMP's).  In every case,
: the latency of the larger machines is higher than the latency of
: the smaller machines, and the penalty is clearly visible in SPECfp95
: performance.
[clip reality]

Looking at Intel chipsets, the server chipsets typically have _longer_
latancies then the desktop chipsets.  The that the larger, more expensive,
chipset has poorer latency is that _bandwidth_ is much more important to a
server (4 processors for Intel) than to a desktop (1 or maybe 2 processors)
when using the same bus.

(Chipset = I/O and memory controler for you non-Intel folks)

There exists a trade-off between latency and bandwidth.  Servers/MP systems
need to sit at a different point on that curve than a Uni-processor.  Server
chipsets have MUCH larger (and slower) buffers.  They must handle MANY more I/O
devices.  They must support, without losing performance, a great deal of
memory.  And of course an Intel server has board layout problems to a much
greater degree than a desktop design.  All of these things _will_ hurt latency,
or at least make keeping latency low more difficult. 

So yes, systems designed to support more processors, I/O and memory
are likely to be slower.  John's data shows this.  My comment is
that this should be expected.  One hopes that the nature of the
workload you will actually run on the server is more latency
tolerant than a desktop application.  (Large database programs
are often multi-threaded and care much more about throughput
than latency for example)

2 cents.

Mark

  




