Not exactly, but the comments below do address the 
pertinent issues.  High bus bandwidth does not require high
latency.  High sustainable per-cpu bandwidth does not require
high latency.  High latency actually *prevents* high sustainable
bandwidth -- as can easily be seen in the STREAM benchmark results.

>They must handle MANY more I/O
>devices.  They must support, without losing performance, a great deal of
>memory.  And of course an Intel server has board layout problems to a much
>greater degree than a desktop design.  All of these things _will_ hurt latency,
>or at least make keeping latency low more difficult. 
>
>So yes, systems designed to support more processors, I/O and memory
>are likely to be slower.  John's data shows this.  

It is true for traditional designs, but need not be true for a
well-designed ccNUMA machine.  The SGI Origin2000, for example,
is designed to accomodate up to 128 cpus, 256 GB RAM, and 192
I/O channels sustaining over 500 MB/s each.  It does this with
quite a reasonable local memory latency (just under 290 ns load-
to-use), independent of machine size.
