Nick Maclaren (nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk) wrote:
: In article <6hvrnl$atm$2@msunews.cl.msu.edu>,
: Mark W Brehob <brehob@cps.msu.edu> wrote:
: >
: >There exists a trade-off between latency and bandwidth.  Servers/MP systems
: >need to sit at a different point on that curve than a Uni-processor.  ...

: I quite take your (snipped) points that it is harder to achieve low
: latency on a complex system, but do you have any references that show
: servers NEED a different latency/bandwidth ratio?

Well remember that in Intel land a MP system and a UP system
have _exactly_ the same pipe to memory.  That is to say that
the bus that is used by a single processor is also used by 
all four processors on an Intel "server" box.  (In this context
I'm only considering it a server if it uses the "Server chipset",
and server chipsets are targeted at 4-way systems). So for the 4-way
system bus utilization becomes _very_ important as many important
applications (database for example) become bus-bandwidth bound.  

Different applications will have different needs.  If the server is acting as a
clasic login-server/print-server then memory bandwidth is less important. (I'd
guess Network bandwidth might be the limiting factor)  If you are running
scientific code then the cache hit rate will determine where the optimal
latency/bandwidth point is.  

Also notice that large systems usually need large I/O and memory subsystems.
Clearly this will increase latency slightly.

: My experience is that the balance is constrained primarily by the
: application and what evidence I have seen points to the fact that most
: servers are nearly as badly constrained by latency as workstations in
: their worst mode.  I.e. it is the "bounce all over the data" programs,
: typical of GUIs and much system/networking code, that are limited by
: latency and little else.

Agreed.  I guess I need to remember that "servers" doesn't just
mean "workhorses running LARGE jobs".  I have a strong Database/
large simulation bias.

