In article <casper.893278294@uk-usenet.uk.sun.com>, Casper.Dik@Holland.Sun.Com (Casper H.S. Dik - Network Security Engineer) writes:
>>Now if clusters are so bad why have Unix vendors been trying to get clustering
>>working for years and why is clustering all the talk of the WNT people.
>
>I don't think anybody said clustering is bad; the world is
>not black and white.  It's just that given the choice between a single,
>pwer ful system and a cluster consisting of less powerful clients, the
>single ssytem will win.  Always.  (When it comes to performance)
>It's a law of nature.
>


This is a very interesting statement. An illogical law of nature ?

Lets take a couple of hypothetical examples.

1) Worlds most powerful single SMP system  X.

   Now take two of them and cluster them - this is hypothetical remember
   so I dont care that whatever system you consider most powerful only runs
   an operating system which cannot be clustered.

   What do we have :-

   Looks like a system which is pretty close to twice as powerful -
   there will be a slight degradation due to cluster communication over a 
   slow interconnect between the two systems but not that much.

   So we now have a clustered system which is more powerful than the most 
   powerful single system.

   Are but maybe your talking price/performance.

   Well you don't need to double up everything eg all your disk storage is
   common. Hence I would suggest price/performance is about the same.


2) Are but you said a cluster of less powerful machines.

   But how much less powerful ?

   How about 3/4 as powerful.


    2 x 3/4  = 1.5  


  As before you lose a bit to cluster communication but only a tiny bit.

  Hence you still have a system more powerful than the most powerful single
  system.

  But these are much less powerful machines - last years models maybe.

  Certainly a lot cheaper (can probably pick them up refurbished or secondhand)

  Hence probably even better on price/performance.


3) How about each system half as powerful.

   Are finally the cluster is less powerful than the single system.

   These are even older systems - even cheaper. Price/performance looks very
   good.

   You might even be able to buy another system and cluster that - oh what 
   that makes it more powerful than the worlds most powerful single system
   again.


The above of course assumes a real world mix of computing jobs.
There are pathological cases in which the above isn't true.
For instance one process with a single thread of execution would not have
access to as much computing power in a cluster as on a single system.
But then such a process would not gain from SMP either.
With a cluster an extension of the above to one process with mutiple
threads occurs however these pathalogical examples will not usually occur
in real life.

So I think your statement needs rewording - something like :-

It's just that given the choice between a single,
powerful system and a two node cluster consisting of systems half as powerful
then the single system will win.  Always.  (When it comes to performance)
It's a law of nature.


Doesn't quite have the same impact does it.



David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader 
Middlesex University



