>In <6hle18$ls4@usenet.pa.dec.com> neideck@kar.dec.com (Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz) writes:
>>if an application is significantly hurt by them, you
>>can build them as a 32bit application on Digital Unix just the same.
>
>Last time I checked, this was much easier said than done.  Command line
>options to the compiler/linker were not enough, the code needed to be
>stuffed with pragmas, otherwise calls to the library functions gave
>segmentation faults.

Correct. It used to be pretty hairy because there aren't any 32-bit
versions of the header files or libraries. Starting in Digital Unix
V4.0D, the C and C++ compilers support a feature called

	-protect_headers

and there is a protect_headers_setup.sh script that makes dealing with
this much simpler. It allows you to set and reset the setting of the
pointers sizes for the library calls more or less automatically.

