In article <6hpkdl$avt@flonk.uk.sun.com> andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com# writes:

<Lots of good discussion deleted>

>A customer can approach me and ask me to architect
>a system that can support say a 6000 user SAP/SD
>population, or a query rate equivalent to say 5000 TPC-D's
>on a 1 TB or 500 GB database and I can design that system
>based on publically available data which the customer
>can verify. 

Mmh. That is not what we usually do. It is true that a customer
ma ask for this, but unless what the customer does *very* closely
matches what TPC-C or TPC-D does, we do measurement and sizing
of the actual workload. TPC-C and -D configurations for benchmarks
are often done without much consideration for backups and reliability
and the characteristics of real workloads often differs considerably
from the exact benchmarks.

>You cannot and any customer dealing with
>Digital will have to be satisfied with assertions like
>the 4100/8400 claim you previously made. As an
>architect I can advise my customer responsibly
>because of this.

If you configure based on the TPC-x numbers without checking into
the customers real application, I would be hesitant as a customer.
True, without published data we cannot size based on TPC-x, but
we'd rather size based on the actual application and commit on
that (which we feel is more accurate anyway).

