Thank you for that opportunity, Maynard, to make an apples-to-apples
comparison.   ;-)

The SGI Origin2000 has list pricing significantly lower than the
DEC 8400, the HP V2250, or the Sun Ultra 10000, for comparable
configurations (same cpu, memory, and I/O).

Some examples:                          SPECfp95 fprate  SPECint95 intrate
Machine        MHz  cpus   RAM   US list    rate   per k$    rate     per k$
SGI Origin2000 250   16    4 GB   $491k     3019    6.2      2088      4.3
HP V2250       240   16    4 GB   $777k     2471    3.2      2209      2.8
Sun UE10000    336   16    4 GB  $1043k    ~2200    2.1      1718      1.6
DEC 8400       612   14    4 GB   $834k    ~1600    1.9      2143      2.6

Notes: 
(1) The Sun SPEC95 rates were estimated as 1/2 of the 32-cpu results,
    and then scaled linearly from the published 250 MHz results to
    the new 336 MHz processors.
(2) The DEC SPECfp_rate results was scaled linearly from 12 to 14 cpus.
    (This is quite generous, since the 12-cpu result is strongly
     bandwidth-limited already, and adding more cpus will just make 
     that contention worse.)

These are, of course, not official quotations, but they are estimates
from an independent third-party consulting firm (except for the SGI
estimates, which are my entirely unofficial readings of the SGI price
book).  These estimates include the O/S, but do not include other software,
maintenance contracts, etc....

Anyway, there is about a 2x price range, and about a 3x price/performance
range.


Pricing on small machines is harder to come by, but here are some 
numbers for 4-cpus in a mid-size server:

Some examples:                          SPECfp95 fprate  SPECint95 intrate
Machine        MHz  cpus   RAM   US list    rate   per k$    rate     per k$
SGI Origin2000 250   4    1 GB    $112k      753    6.7       523      4.7
HP K580        240   4    1 GB    $197k      532    2.7       610      3.1
Sun UE3000     336   4    1 GB    $112k     ~668    6.0      ~498      4.4

and here for 4 cpus in a non-expandible server:

Some examples:                          SPECfp95 fprate  SPECint95 intrate
Machine        MHz  cpus   RAM   US list    rate   per k$    rate     per k$
SGI Origin200  180   4    1 GB    $ 56k      559   10.0       389      6.9
Sun UE450      296   4    1 GB    $ 62k      561    9.0       422      6.8
DEC 4100       533   4    1 GB    $113k      565    5.0       575      5.1

Notes:
(1) I don't have pricing on the new DEC 4100 with 600 MHz cpus and 8 MB
    L3 caches, so I used price and performance data for the 533 MHz system
    (which is clearly not a slouch).
(2) The Sun UE3000 specrate numbers were estimated from the 6-cpu results
    at 250 MHz.  (Multiply by 2/3, then scale linearly from 250 to 335 MHz).

