Like I said earlier don't assume that Galaxies are in anyway unique. Some
hard lessons for you:

1.    Remember the Sequent lesson don't compare your
      new platform with your old platfom instead compare
      it with the best in the market. If you see any internal
      documents on Galaxies ask yourself, are they making
      comparisions between our current products or are
      these being compared against Sun/HP etc.

2.    Being 4x as fast as the current 8400 would not even
       put Digital on top of the current system heap. Given
       the HP and Sun systems roadmap assume that
      10x faster isn't going to be nearly good enough
      either.

3.    Remember that the biggest single image Digital
       systems in production are at best 12 CPU systems
       don't assume that when you start putting 32/64
       CPU's together that they will scale or that
       application will scale.

4.    Be prepared to spend millions of dollars with
       Oracle/SAP/Baan/Sybase/informix etc engineering
       to fix wrinkles in their software and your OS
       before they do work properly at the levels
       suggested for Galaxies.

5.    Be prepared to wait 1-2 years before customers
       show much interest in Galaxies and before they
       work as advertised.

6.    Remember that no one is really interested in
       OpenVMS so announcing Galaxies with
       OpenVMS support but with DU/NT support
       to follow will not make the impression you
       are hoping for.

7.    Finally remember the words of Andy Heller
       the origional RS/6000 architect. "Technology
       is like fish, the longer you leave it on the
       shelf the less attractive it is"


Regards
