>
> Maybe this is a difference of terminology but what do you mean by
> partitioning a database ???
>
> A clustered datatabase is just one database. This is not a distributed database
> in which you have parts split over different machines.
>

Certainly it is but in order to avoid things likelock pinging in Oracle which will hurt your
performance you need to do more than
just plonk the database down on the cluster.

> Please can you explain what you mean.
>

You need to partition your clients and theirtables. For example if you have two nodes
in a cluster you need to point half the clients
to one node and half to the other.

You may think this is trivial but actually even
this can be fraught with issues.

Next you need to ensure that the clients on
node A do not access tables/rows that are
being accessed by clients on node B. If they
do then the lock pinging effects start and these
can hurt your performance.

In order to do the above you need ideally to partiton
your database.

Note there is still one database as you origionally
envisaged but you are trying to ensure that
the two nodes in your cluster don't lock records
that the other node may want access to.

This all looks trivial but it isn't. Particularly when
you add an application layer like SAP on top
which may have its own connection layer which
does not respond well to your attempts at client
partitioning.

If you doubt this ask a SAP/IBM SP2 customer
how they are getting on with their SAP cluster.

Now factor in 8 nodes rather than 2 and your
problem has just got a whole load more exciting.

Roll in a few small hot tables that can't be partitioned
easily which all the clients need access to for example
in the Baan environment the Baan0 tables and
hey presto you have a non scaling cluster.

These above reasons are why everyone had
such a good laugh over Microsofts pathetic
scalability day benchmarks.


Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect






