Nick Maclaren (nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk) wrote:
: Actually, the arguments don't change at all!  All that happens is that the
: timescale changes.  This has a major effect on which applications are
: affected, and how badly, but the general problems and solutions are almost
: the same.  Someone pointed out that the 'critical packet size' (i.e.
: latency*bandwidth) of modern memory is approaching that of 1970s disk,
: and that of modern networks is already comparable.

Agreed, but we are creating another thread ;)  This latency/bandwidth thing inside the box
argument is interesting, but from my *limited* (no big compile/render/number crunching shops),
it seems to me real business world applications/machines (both Unix and W) are getting I/O
bound at the disk.  When I am twiddling my thumbs at home (W95) my disk is trashing, when i
twiddle my thumbs at work (SS20, 128M), the disk is trashing (man, CDE and NS4.04 are mem pigs
:(

I don't really read much of the benchmark info.  Don't trust benchmarks/specs.  You won't ever
see 10Mbit consistently on shared segment (not to mention how fast it starts to choke when you
actually use it), but it *is* 10Mbit. End user frustration is the best benchmark ;)

On the network side, I haven't seen any commercial/corporate apps (voice over IP would be
kewl, but haven't seen any companies using it) that really make the latency vs. bandwidth
thing an issue.  I know there are situations out there (moving BIG digital images, etc), but
it seems to me most users/networks need more bandwidth (at clogged points) than less latency. 
When things choke it is because the pipe is too small, not cause sessions aren't snappy
enough.  And given the drastically dropping prices in network gear, switched 100MBit to the
desktop is not too far.  Man, how could end users even dream of using that pipe?

Bandwidth is getting *real* cheap.  Especially for LAN's.

--
Scott Walters
Packet Pusher




