>> >> vendor model latency SPECfp95          model latency SPECfp95
>> >> DEC    8400   ~400   20.8 @ 612MHz     4100   ~250   25.1 @ 612MHz (1)
>> >> HP     V2250  500?   24.8 @ 240MHz     K580    ???   28.5 @ 240MHz (2)
>> >> Sun    UE10K  ~500   11.3 @ 250MHz (3) UE450  ~200   15.0 @ 250MHz
>> >> -----------------------------------------------------------------------
>> >> SGI    Origin  290   24.5 @ 250MHz     Origin  290   24.5 @ 250MHz
>> >> -----------------------------------------------------------------------
>
> John, could you expand the "big machine latency" column to show
> the difference between process working set small enough to live
> on one node and large enough to require distribution over several
> nodes?

I am not sure that my ASCII artwork is up to the task (since
this rapidly becomes multi-dimensional), but I can describe
the behavior, anyway....

On the SGI Origin2000 (or other ccNUMA machines), a trade-off is
made between local and remote accesses.  Local accesses can be
made significantly faster, but as a consequence, remote accesses
become somewhat more complicated (at the hardware level), and
therefore slower.

On the 250 MHz Origin2000, the formula for latency is 290 ns
for local access, plus 160 ns for leaving the node, plus 100 ns
for each router traversed (50 ns for the outgoing request
plus 50 ns for the incoming data).

The Origin2000 is built using a "bristled hypercube" topology,
which minimizes the number of router traversals required.  For
example, the 16-cpu machine discussed has a maximum of 3 router
traversals for the worst case.  We also offer the option of
"express links" using one of the extra router ports to reduce the
worst case to 2 router traversals.  But just to keep the
discussion general, I will look at the standard case.

The 16-cpu Origin2000 has 4 routers arranged in a square, and
each router has 2 nodes attached to it.  Each "node" has some
memory and 2 cpus.

The quantity of memory at each "distance" is:
	local		1 node
	1 router	1 node
	2 routers	4 nodes
	3 routers	2 nodes

So for data that is completely uniformly distributed, the
average latency is:
	1/8*(
	 1*(290)
	+1*(290+160+100)
	+4*(290+160+200)
	+2*(290+160+300) )
	----------------
	(290 + 550 + 4*650 + 2*750)/8 = 4940/8 = 618 ns.

So there is some penalty (relative to a "flat" SMP system) if
you pay no attention to locality at all, and just get random
placement of the data.  Note that this is a small penalty 
relative to the "big" Sun UE10000 (at ~500 ns), but a large
penalty relative to the "mid-sized" Sun UE6000 (at ~250 ns).

There are a variety of operating system mechanisms in place
to give you better locality than this (for example, each job
gets pages locally first, and if those run out, it gets pages
from near nodes before distant ones), however if you are 
using all the memory in the machine for a single process, the
average latency will be as shown above.

For unit-stride accesses, the bandwidth to/from remote memory is
closer to being uniform than the latency.  For example, a
"bcopy()" operation with one local reference and one remote
reference (3 routers away) runs at about 88% of the performance
of the case with both local references.  The absolute rate for
bcopy therefore varies between 146 MB/s for all local accesses
down to 130 MB/s for "one-operand-three-hops-away" to about
105 MB/s for "both-operands-three-hops-away".  This can be
compared to 82 MB/s for the "flat" Sun Ultra 10000 server,
and 140 MB/s for the smaller "flat" Sun Ultra 6001 server.  (Here
I am using the "bcopy()" convention for counting bytes -- to get
STREAM Copy numbers, double these figures).

The cases with contention are more complicated, and I will 
save that discussion for another time....


To summarize, ccNUMA machines optimize for local memory
performance at a slight penalty for global random-access latency,
but not necessarily for remote access bandwidth.  The benefit is
superior local memory latency and bandwidth, and decreased cost
(i.e., no need to buy a big crossbar/switch/bus).
-- 
