> 3.    Remember that the biggest single image Digital
>        systems in production are at best 12 CPU systems
>        don't assume that when you start putting 32/64
>        CPU's together that they will scale or that
>        application will scale.

Presumably if it were just a matter of "assuming" things
would scale, Galaxy would be in our hands by now.   When
DEC has work to do to make things scale, that is when
there is the best opportunity for leadership scaling.
Based on the incremental benchmarks which have been posted
here for adding extra CPUs to large multiprocessors systems
from DEC's competitors, there is a lot of room left for
leadership in scaling.

I would "assume" there will be applications where Galaxy provides
leadership scaling, and that there will be applications where it
does not.   Presumably the same is true for Sun leadership in
some metric or another.  Anytime someone suggests that some
computer-related postulate is _always_ true, I start mistrusting.

> 6.    Remember that no one is really interested in
>        OpenVMS so announcing Galaxies with
>        OpenVMS support but with DU/NT support
>        to follow will not make the impression you
>        are hoping for.

Certainly nobody at Sun is interested in OpenVMS, because they have
nothing to compete with it.  (This disinterest extends so far as to
cause misunderstanding of the nature of a Cluster.)  Even before
actions by Compaq, I bet the people at DEC and Tandem understood
each other's strengths and weaknesses a lot better than those
who cannot understand any operating system but Unix (or Windows,
for that matter).

If your only tool is Unix, then every problem begins to look like
a nail.  Though I don't care much for Unix or Windows NT, I know
that others do, and I would never suggest that one of those two
will prevail over the other, since the last time "everybody"
favored the same computing environment was just before the second
computing environment was created.

Larry Kilgallen



