In article <y4hg3k7r9b.fsf@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de>, Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> writes:
|> jonathan@Cup.DSG.Stanford.EDU (Jonathan Stone) writes:
|> 
|> >    b) using  NetBSD's support for  splitting drivers
|> >       into a per-bus front-end and a per-chipset backend.
|> 
|> Sounds similar to VMS' (still continuing) move to the class driver/port driver
|> split, first pioneered by the terminal driver guys, IIRC. Is it?

Not very. Unix had a split between tty-level and device-level drivers
for decades.


This is more like supporting a DZ-11/dc6??/dc7085 device with a single
chipset driver, regardless of whether it's on a Unibus, a Q-bus (easy
enough) or a Vaxstation motherboard (not so hard) or a Pmax
motherboard (different address-decoding on DZ registers).

Or using the same Lance chipset driver for all of:

	* vaxstation baseboard
	* PMAX (3100/2100) baseboard
	  (again, funky 16-to-32-bit DMA padding)
	* ioasic baseboard Ethernet device on TC Alphas and decstatoins
	  (even funkier   16-byte-out-of-32-byte DMA padding)
	* PMAZ-AA Turbochannel option slot
	* Lance devices on Sun workstation mnotherboards,
	* lance devices on hp300 workstations,
	* various ISA, PCI,  other LANCE-compatile cards...

The VMS port/class driver cut comes at a different layer.  I'd compare
that split to NetBSD's "scsi" layer, which has a dozen or so drivers
with the "scsi" attribute under it, the SCSI (and ATAPI) machinery
itself, and then disk, tape, scanner, and jukebox drivers on top.

Of course, I havent written VMS device drivers since 4.6 or 5.1, so
caveat emptor.



