> as a "DEC 2000 Model 300 AXP - ROM Version 2.2".
> 
> I'm trying to start the loading of VMS by booting from the CD-ROM, but
> I'm getting an error.  (This same external CD-ROM drive worked for
> loading firmware.)  The following is what occurs:
> 
>  >>> boot dka200
>  INIT-S-CPU ...
>  AUDIT_BOOT_STARTS ...
> 
>    84 FAIL
> 
>  >>>
> 
> I've checked the setting of some things like boot_osflags, bootdef_dev,
> and os_type.  I have also upgraded the firmware and run the EISA config
> utility.
> 
> Does anyone have a suggestion of what else I might be able to change or
> look at?

I just tried it on our AXP 150 with the OpenVMS 7.1 CD and didn't have
any problems.  (boot_osflags is set to 0,0)

There are a few of things to consider.

First off, SRM 2.2 is the latest (probably last ever) firmware for this
box.  So that looks OK.

The 2000-300 usually (always?) shipped with an RRD42 CDROM as DKA400. 
Are you sure that DKA200 is the correct boot device?

Check and double check your termination.  If you have added the CDROM
externally, use as short a cable as you can.  Try to use 3' or shorter. 
The SCSI controller in the 2000-300 (an AHA1742) has been reported to be
touchy about having both external and internal connections.  Some people
have reported never getting such a configuration to work at all.

If you don't have the original CDROM drive, you may have a drive which
is not compatible with VMS.  I have had pretty good luck with Sony (the
RRD42 is really a Sony CDU-541) and Toshiba CDROMs.  I have had bad luck
with NEC drives, but haven't tried one in years, so they may have fixed
their problems.

What you need is a CDROM which supports 512 byte sectors.  Many which
were designed for the PC market only support 2048 byte sectors.  You
should also enable parity.

Good luck!

