> roughly equivalent (for our purposes, at least).  I don't remember exactly
> how much we paid for the K460 about a year ago, but it was a _lot_.

Sad that HP is still doing this.  No wonder they think Intel is the
future - they can't control their own costs.  I wonder how they will
be able to sustain their margins after moving to Intel...

A few years ago I was in a software development organization that
had some Sun, HP, IBM, etc. and they needed a new mid-sized server.
After the big-shots got done with their kissing party with HP it
came down that we *had* to buy a K<something>.  This was when it
first came out.  I think it was around $75K.  Some of us groaned
at the amount of money and what it could buy us in Sun gear.  After
we get it we find out that, for software development, it
supported less workload than our little SPARCserver 10 with 2
processors!  (That we might have paid $25K for a year
or two earlier.)

That was sad but not as bad as a joke coming a few months later -
an AT&T (NCR) server.  8 Pentium processors, 1/2 GB RAM, big
RAID array, batteries...  worth over 1/2 a million.  Another
"business" kissing party where AT&T loaned us the machine to port
our product - and our management agreed that we would be moving
*ALL* our primary development to that box!!!  When we moved it
into our computer room we named in "anchor" because of the
weight but it took only a day or two to realize that we had
also hit the nail on the head regarding its value for doing
anything.  Even *free* it was worthless!  The business world
is so much fun!


