In article <01bd6f73$c7d9bde0$3ef010ac@UKCND0447.cnk.uk.origin-it.com>, 
    	"Andrew Jones" <andrew.jones@NOSPAMuk.origin-it.com> writes:
> Hi All,
> this is the first time I've posted to a newsgroup, so if I'm in the wrong
> one I'm sure someone will point me in the right direction.
> 
> I have two questions about licences:
> 1)
> I have a quote for QL-36NAE-AA. The spd shows QL-36NE*-** and refers to a
> 'price book'. Where can I find a 'price book' or some other explanation of
> the *-** in the product number.

        I believe Joe Huber or  someone  else followed-up on this one...
    Nevertheless,  the  first part is a "Traditional" license  for  "NAS
    Client 150" which gives covers things like UCX, Motif, DECNET,  etc.
    On  the  Digital Business link, it lists for US$1542.  There are two
    other suffixes, -RA is for an "Update License", US$456, while -ZG is
    for a trade-in (or  trade-down?)  from another system, US$386...  If
    this is simply a newly-acquired system, you need the QL-36NAE-AA.

> 2)
> I have a second user AlphaServer 1000a 4/266 which currently has nothing
> installed on it (blank disks!). I have purchased a 16 user OpenVMS licence
> (QL-MT3AA-3F) and I have been told I need QL-MT1AE-6R aswell. Is this
> right?

        When  you  purchased  the  AlphaServer  1000A,  you  would  have
    purchased  it  _with_ an operating system license for either VMS  or
    DU, presumably for VMS.  This is the _base_ VMS license.  You need a
    "VMS USER" license as well, which  can  come  in  many  sizes.   You
    obviously  have  the  16-user  version of the VMS USER license.  You
    also need the base VMS license, which is what the QL-MT1AE-6R is.

        Check your purchasing documents, then  get  DEC to issue you the
    PAK.  It's very frequently the case (in my experience) that the PAKs
    are  delivered  quite  a  bit  later than  the  system  hardware  is
    delivered (like that makes  a  lot  of  sense!).   But  if  you  can
    reference your P.O., or better, the "DEC Number" on the packing slip
    that  came  with the system, DEC will FAX you a temporary PAK within
    24 hours (at least in the US).   If you purchased the AS1000 through
    a  DEC reseller, bang on their heads to get you the PAKs and  refuse
    to pay for the system until they deliver it!   [This  on  the  basis
    that the system, as delivered, is inoperable without the PAKs.]

        -Ken
