Problems, problems, problems, Windows NT is full of problems.

1. When you install Windows NT and you might only be running Office and
nothing else, ensure that you set the page file size during installation
such that the initial size equals the maximum size. This will force
Windows NT to allocate a pagefile of the maximum size, usually it is
contiguous. If you plan to run SQL or other stuff, the it pays to plan
your disk storage much better, and in this case, you are best to move the
pagefile to a FAT formatted non-system disk and maximise the size as
appropriate, with the use of PerfMon to help.

2. Windows NT when using NTFS will allocate files to the disk, leaving
space in between files for files to grow. Kind of funny, I had always
wondered if MS wrote self-modifying code, now I wonder even more. Any how,
when it winds up at the end of the disk, it then tris to allocate storage
for files, in amongst all htose other files. If you wnat to see graphic
evidence of the heavy fragmentation this results in, install Windows NT on
a 4 GB disk, get a demo of Disk Keeper Lite and see how badly fragmented
that 4 GB disk drive is, and all you did was install Windows NT. I find
that at about the yesr os use mark, the disk is so bad, I wipe it and
re-install.

3. NT is always self-tuning, so in fact, it's never really in tune, and
I'll bet that at any particular instance, it's probably 10 to 20% badly
tuned, due to self tuning, than what I can do with a OpenVMS machine over
six month period. In other words, we OpenVMS System Manager are worth more
that those MSCE, because we actually know what to do and how and
understand it too.

4. Applications crash, and although they do not show up on the desktop,
browsing through Task Manager may surprise you.

5. I have a AlphaServer 4100 with 2GB of memory and lots of disk. Over a
three week period, it get's noticeably slower and slower to log into. I
reboot it. That happens to be the standard Microsoft fix for this and most
other problems, and if you have to use Windows NT, you have to learn to
live with and accept the reboot as a real bonifide troubleshooting fixall.
I know, I am OpenVMS educated and I tell you it's hard, it's real hard to
grasp this and accept, but, there is nothing else to do.

rtt

