> Hi, all.  Three quick yes/no questions for those who are using Alphaserver and
> AlphaStation systems (such as the 1000A and 2100A for servers, and the 200
> 4/266 or 600 5/333 or even the new Personal Workstation 433au series):
> 
> 1) Can these systems be run without a graphics monitor on them, the way we
> used to run DEC 3000/300 units?  That is, can the console be a simple terminal
> connected to a system serial port and the system run without a monitor,
> keyboard, or mouse attached?  (I need to be able to hook these systems up to a
> VaxCluster Console System via an old DECserver).

Only if you run either OpenVMS Alpha or Digital UNIX.
Windows NT requires a Graphics Console. Who said PC's were cheaper????


> 
> 2) Do I understand correctly that for Alpha systems there is a difference
> in the firmware on the motherboard depending on whether you are running
> VMS/UNIX or NT?  Can any OS run on any unit as long as the firmware is
> upgraded, or are some systems locked to a particular OS?  Can one change
> thee firmware (after buying a CD presumably) on any of these units?

The principal firmware difference is, SRM console for UNIX or OpenVMS and
ARC/AlphaBIOS console for Windows NT. As the interupt architecture is
fundementally being changed here by the different PAL code, any system
with a EISA bus mandates the running of the proper ECU, either the
OpenVMS, UNIX on or the Windows NT one, prior to booting the OS.
Firmware is readily available in the latest versions at, www.digital.com


