In article <6hpadq$le2$1@alanya.m.isar.de>,
Lupe Christoph <lupe@alanya.m.isar.de> wrote:
>
>If you could quote a figure how "putting the memory the other side of
>a bus" increased latency, I could start arguing about that. 

There is a lot of suggestive data out there, if you know how
to look at it....

Several vendors offer large SMP machines using the same cpus as
in smaller machines (uniprocessors or small SMP's).  In every case,
the latency of the larger machines is higher than the latency of
the smaller machines, and the penalty is clearly visible in SPECfp95
performance.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  	     big machine		    small machine
vendor	model latency SPECfp95		model latency SPECfp95
DEC	8400   ~400   20.8 @ 612MHz     4100   ~250   25.1 @ 612MHz (1)
HP	V2250  500?   24.8 @ 240MHz	K580	???   28.5 @ 240MHz (2)
Sun	UE10K  ~500   11.3 @ 250MHz (3) UE450  ~200   15.0 @ 250MHz
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SGI	Origin  290   24.5 @ 250MHz     Origin  290   24.5 @ 250MHz
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Notes:
(1) This result is estimated from the 466 MHz and 533 MHz results,
    assuming that SPECfp/MHz would remain fixed for a hypothetical
    612 MHz 4100 with a 4 MB L3 cache.  The published DEC result of
    29.2 is higher because of the use of an 8 MB L3 cache on that 
    system.  So, John Henning, how close is my estimate?

    Note that the latency numbers here are from lmbench, and 
    are not directly comparable to the latency numbers for the
    other platform.  The important thing to note is the ratio
    between the "small platform" latency and the "large platform"
    latency.

(2) I can't find my latency numbers for the HP K580 machine.
    As I recall, the figure is about 200-250 ns.  The V2250
    number is estimated to be about the same as the Exemplar S
    number.   Performance results suggest that the V-series
    machines have improved the latency somewhat, but the results
    suggest that it is unlikely that the latency is down to the
    level of the K580.  Any numbers from HP-land?

(3) This is an extrapolated result based on the published SPECfp_rate
    result of 3280 on 32 cpus.   The conversion is to divide the result
    by the number of processors to get SPECfp_rate per processor and
    then to divide by 9 to get the same units as SPECfp95.  (Try this
    on uniprocessor SPECfp_rate results -- it is exact.)  If the UE10K
    is already hitting scaling trouble by 32 cpus, then the single-
    cpu number might be a bit higher, but the published SPECfp_rate
    vs cpu count for the UE10K appears pretty linear in the 32-48 cpu
    range, and only drops off a bit at 64 cpus, so I am guessing 
    that this is pretty close.  So, Bradley Carlile and company,
    how close is my estimate?



In summary, DEC loses 17% of uniprocessor SPECfp95 due to the
increased latency of the larger system, Sun loses 25%, and HP
loses 13%.  The SGI results are in there to show that a correctly
designed ccNUMA machine has no uniprocessor performance penalty
when running in large configurations.






>So I'll
>just say that having a known uniform latency versus am unknown,
>varying latency helps you quite a bit. 

There is plenty of data that suggests that this is not true.
What I posted above is just a small piece.  Look around for
yourself at publicly available performance data -- the small
low-latency machines always beat the "big iron" with the 
same cpu and cache....


>The STREAM benchmark shows that with hand-tuned code, you can
>overload the backplane, but not with FORTRAN code. 

For the particular case of the Sun Ultra 4000/5000/6000 that
has been the subject of this thread, this assertion is
completely incorrect.   Just look at the scalability of the
SPECfp_rate results for the high-bandwidth codes (tomcatv,
swim, and mgrid).  Speedups on those codes are:

		fp_rate  fp_rate
	code	1-copy	30-copies	Speedup
	tomcatv	  240     2082		  8.7x
	swim	  283	  2993		 10.6x
	mgrid	  119	  1949	         16.4x

If it is not possible to "overload the backplane" with Fortran
codes, then why is it that running 30 independent copies of these
codes doesn't give a 30x improvement in throughput?   The answer
is that (by a wide margin) the system memory interconnect does not
provide enough bandwidth.
