Benchmarks can be useful, but only to the extent that they mirror 
the characteristics of your type of workload. TPC obviously doesn't
mean spit if you use your systems for something computationally 
intense like 3-D rendering, for instance.

The other problem that you get into is that vendors will often 'stack'
the benchmarks. A classic case was the time when HP started using
fortran
preprocessors to optimize certain loops out of SPEC FP benchmarks. Their 
numbers skyrocketed, not because they were really running faster, but
because
they had eliminated much of the code from the executable. While that may
be
a valid thing for those few people whose code matched the SPEC code
closely,
the problem is that it was perceived as a wildly misleading improvement
in
general FP performance.

Similar things go on in TPC. Look at the full disclosure for the IBM SP2
numbers, for instance. On one of their reported tests, you'll find that
the
test system was configured with over 800 disk drives, at a VERY low
utilization
per drive - obviously an attempt to artificially drive up the I/O
bandwidth in
a way that real customers would never do. Also, the TPC benchmarks will
often 
run entirely in cache on machines that have large L2 or L3 caches. Real
customer applications rarely do, and for that reason performance on
customer
applications is often dramatically lower that the published TPC numbers
would
suggest.

Back in the SPARCstation 2 days, I was involved in a customer benchmark
against
some old DECstation stuff. According to published SPEC and graphics
numbers from
both vendors, the Sun stuff was about 20% faster. At the customer site,
we blew the
Sun stuff away performance wise. Why? Well, if you read the fine print
on the Sun
brochures, you discovered that the reported SPEC numbers were produced
with 
experimental compilers that were not commercially available at the time,
and that the
graphics numbers reported in the glossies (3-d triangles per second,
etc.) were raw
frame buffer numbers, and did NOT represent the actual performance
through X - as
did the published Digital numbers.

All I can say is that published benchmarks should always be taken with a
grain of
salt. I would really recommend that customers ALWAYS try to benchmark
with their
real application, if possible. And I suppose I'm biased, but one of the
things
that I've always liked about Digital is that they don't tend to publish
benchmarks
that mis-represent the performance of their hardware. Not every vendor
can say that.
Perhaps it's not smart marketing, but it's ethical, and in my mind
that's more
important.

Finally, and hopefully without getting sucked into a meaningless flame
war with 
Mr. Harrison of Sun UK, I will just finish with this. Pretty much
invariably, 
when we benchmark head-to-head with real customer applications, we beat
our
competitors. Because of historically ... um, let's say "less than
stellar"
marketing, we unfortunately don't alwasy get invited to the party. 

That's improving, though - I've worked with Digital on and off since
1989, and I
can tell you categorically that I'm moving more big iron now than I've
ever moved
before. I've dropped 8400s into a bunch of new accounts here in my city
of Columbus,
Ohio, USA over the last year, and more than half have gone into accounts
that
have NEVER bought Digital before. The benefits (quite real, I assure
you) of 
VLM have won those deals in highly competitive situations. And I can
also tell
you that I was personally involved in the sale of many dozens of 8000
class
systems to a long time Sun customer (can't name names, I'm under
non-disclosure).
Why? Because despite all of the "bandwidth" discussion that Mr. Harrison
is so
fond of aside, we slaughtered the Sun gear on real TCP/IP throughput at
the
applications level. That's also why 72 of the 100 largest Web sites run
on our
stuff.

We do need bigger boxes. I think I can safely say that's going to be
addressed
pretty soon. And the momentum behind Digital UNIX is growing quickly.
Keep your
eyes open for announcements. Bottom line is that we not only aren't just
going
away, our business is doing quite well - and I think it will soon be
doing quite
a bit better.

Methinks, Andrew, that the gentleman doth protest too much. If we really
are 
of no concern to you, I can't imagine why a busy systems architect would
spend
the amount of time that you obviously spend trying to talk down our
products. Lord
knows I don't have the time (or, truth to tell, the inclination - I find
the kind
of mud-slinging that's been going on here tacky, at best). Oh, I have
plenty of 
ammunition, since I managed a 400 seat Solaris shop for two years.
