david20@alpha1.mdx.ac.uk (D.Webb) writes:

>   Big deal - you run UCX$CONFIG for the first and Autogen for the second.


So it is mor ework than setting up a single system, right?

>2) Database vendors such as Oracle have already done all the work you need to 
>   support clustering and this does not involve 'partitioning databases'.
>   The database is just as you would set it up for a single node.
>   The only difference is that each system has to startup its own instance 
>   to access the database - this has it's own rollback segments and redo logs.
>   However the instances on the two or more clustered systems access exactly
>   the same database files and use the distributed lock manager to co-ordinate
>   this access.

True enough, but performance does go out of the window when the patitioning
breaks.  Even now, with something as pertitionable as TPC_C, you see
that the numbers do not scale well with cluser nodes added.

>Now if clusters are so bad why have Unix vendors been trying to get clustering
>working for years and why is clustering all the talk of the WNT people.

I don't think anybody said clustering is bad; the world is
not black and white.  It's just that given the choice between a single,
pwer ful system and a cluster consisting of less powerful clients, the
single ssytem will win.  Always.  (When it comes to performance)
It's a law of nature.

That doesn't mean clusters are bad, but it does mean that clusters
are a last resort when you need more performance.  Just like need
MP instead of a single processor is not good.  A single processor
with everythin N time sbetter than N slower processors, will always be
faster. Also a law of nature.

>Galaxies on the otherhand are not clusters. A Galaxy machine will have multiple
>copies of the operating system running on multiple CPUs in the same box.
>In some ways this is similar to a cluster with the systems memory acting as the
>cluster interconnect. Galaxies are already here - they have been setup and are
>running on 8400s at a number of Digital sites. However the real benefit will
>come when you can have much larger machines with more memory and cpus.

So what is thi slike, much like ccNUMA (or without the CC)?  or MPP?
This description itself doesn't strike me as very new.
Or like starfire domains, but then implemented in software?

>This is where wildfire comes in - but at the moment this is still vapourware.
>However if it comes off then Sun and a number of other vendors will need to
>lookout. A 72 Cpu wildfire with 200GB odd of memory with the SMP CPU usability
>scaling practically linearly because of the galaxy technology will kick some
>arse.


Linear scaling, really, on all workloads?  You gotta be kidding.

>I know - noone will ever buy it - why would you need that power ?
>Now where have I heard that before ?

>Could it be :-

>A 64 bit operating system - why would you need that ?

Come off it already.  That's not at all the same thing.  We wouldn't
be shipping 64 CPU /64GB memory systems and even bigger clusters of
same if power wasn't what people wanted.  (72 processors w/ 200GB
memory doesn't strike me as particularly big for a next generation system)

Besides, nobody ever said that you don't need 64 bits; it's just tat
most people really don't need it.  Most, not all.

We'll judge the systems when they get out and when TCP-C and TCP-D numbers
have been published (and SAP and BAAN etc)

>Well Oracle seemed to show that access to a large memory via 64 bits has this
>way of improving performance.

On what systems?  What benchmarks?  At this point we see a 32 bit
system leading the TCP-D 1000GB benchmark and you would'nt argue that
that is a lot of data, would you?

And 32 bit Solaris its own on the TCp_C benchmarks too.

>Now who is developing 64 bit operating systems ?

Everybody who hasn't got one yet?  I think everybody agrees that there
are advantages to 64 bit flat address space computing.

Casper
