> However if it comes off then Sun and a number of other vendors will need to
> lookout. A 72 Cpu wildfire with 200GB odd of memory with the SMP CPU usability
> scaling practically linearly because of the galaxy technology will kick some
> arse.

Maybe but as I said in my origional post don't assumethat Galaxies are in anyway unique.

> I know - noone will ever buy it - why would you need that power ?
> Now where have I heard that before ?
>
> Could it be :-
>
> A 64 bit operating system - why would you need that ?
>
> Well Oracle seemed to show that access to a large memory via 64 bits has this
> way of improving performance.
>

No Oracle did not, Digital marketing using embarasinglybogus benchmark analysis did. Subsequently Digital
have proved unable to substantiate these claims using
any standard benchmarks like TPC-C to TPC-D. Nor
have they been able to suppy a box that will combine
a decent number of CPU's, enough memory to make
VLDB work and enough I/O because of the
CPU, Memory, I/O tradeoffs required in the 8400.

> Now who is developing 64 bit operating systems ?

No one has ever said 64 bit is bad, but very littleactual evidence exists that VLDB64 has delivered
