John W. Manly wrote:
> 
> Hi, all.  Three quick yes/no questions for those who are using Alphaserver and
> AlphaStation systems (such as the 1000A and 2100A for servers, and the 200
> 4/266 or 600 5/333 or even the new Personal Workstation 433au series):
> 
> 1) Can these systems be run without a graphics monitor on them, the way we
> used to run DEC 3000/300 units?  That is, can the console be a simple terminal
> connected to a system serial port and the system run without a monitor,
> keyboard, or mouse attached?  (I need to be able to hook these systems up to a
> VaxCluster Console System via an old DECserver).

Yes, I have 2 Alphaserver 1000A's running without just VT220 clones for
the console.
OK the console firmware complains about the keyboard not being plugged
in on
power up, but it works fine. At a previous site I had the same config,
but we had
to plug in the monitor to run the RAID config I think (thats 18months
ago now, memory
is getting a little hazy).

> 
> 2) Do I understand correctly that for Alpha systems there is a difference
> in the firmware on the motherboard depending on whether you are running
> VMS/UNIX or NT?  Can any OS run on any unit as long as the firmware is
> upgraded, or are some systems locked to a particular OS?  Can one change
> thee firmware (after buying a CD presumably) on any of these units?

Turbochannel systems cannot run WNT. The systems I have used that are
capable of running NT have both the ARC (NT) and SRM (VMS/DU) consoles
loaded, one uses a simple command to switch between them. Some Alphas 
are WNT only, and so presumably do not have SRM console.

> 
> 3) As an educational site in the CSLG program, we get free access to the
> OSF-USR license PAK, that permits unlimited users onto any system we have
> running DEC UNIX.  Is there any reason that anyone knows of why we wouldn't be
> able to do the same thing on an Alphaserver 1000A or a Personal Workstation
> 433au system, and thus use the system for timesharing purposes?  We're looking
> for something more powerful than our aging DEC 3000/700 system to provide
> basic UNIX timesharing services for students.

I installed a OSF-USR pak on a AS 1000A runing DU 3.2x (can't remember
what x
was now, again this was 18 months ago) with no problems. That was a
DECCamapus site
too. Why are you worried that your CLSG licences are tied to your older
3000
series h/w?

> 
> Thanks for any help or pointers to further information!
>

You are welcome.
 
-- 
Tim Llewellyn, OpenVMS System Manager, Remarcs Project 
MedAS at the BBC, Whiteladies Road, Bristol, UK. 
