Funny you should mention that.  Here we are, an HP shop, and the need has
come up for a new low/midrange box to run a database front-end product on.
It'll probably be a Sun E450.  HP isn't even in the running, though we
did consider upgrading our K460 instead of purchasing a new system, until 
we saw the price. Maybe next time, HP.

>They better pull out of this nose-dive quick - they have let their
>server dominance fade and their workstations get weaker.  I imagine
>that the strategy of dumping PA-RISC and moving to Merced is not
>helping either.

What really doesn't help is HP's outrageous pricing.  Usually they are
about 2.5 times the price of roughly equivalent Sun systems.  Maybe it's
worth it to AT&T or Ford to get that extra bit of floating point, but
smaller customers (like us, a small state government agency) are on a
budget!  

Let's see, I can pay over US$30,000 for one 180MHz PA-8000 board (1 MB
cache) and a 1024 MB RAM upgrade for my HP K460, or I can pay less than
US$30,000 for a complete Sun E450 with 300 MHz UltraSPARC (2 MB cache),
1024 MB RAM, and the standard disk, CD, 10/100 ethernet (the K460 came
with 10 Mb only), 10 PCI slots (compared to the K's 1 proprietary HSC
slot, 4 more if you get the expansion, and no I won't count PB slots as
anything other than a waste of space), and Solaris (I won't argue whether
HP-UX or Solaris is better--neither ships with source so who cares).  

I know upgrades on that level are generally expensive but when you can buy
a whole system for less and do the job better too, it's ridiculous.  And
yes, the MHz numbers don't mean anything, my point was that the chips were
roughly equivalent (for our purposes, at least).  I don't remember exactly
how much we paid for the K460 about a year ago, but it was a _lot_.

