In article <qxpbttwtm1m.fsf@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>,
Dirk Grunwald  <grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu> wrote:
> Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com#> writes:
> > Pardon me for asking but why do you need >4GB address space fora web server. They
> > generally use UFS and the UFS buffer cache
> > can be as big as you want it to be.
> --
> 
> Actually, that's a design flaw introduced by the use of machines
> without support for large memory. Typically, the proxy server would
> want to manage the memory space themselves

Every time I see this argument I reach for my bogometer.  Yes, it's true
that _some_ code, with some _very_ special needs, can do a better job of
cache management than either mapping a plain file in and relying on UFS
or, if they want don't want persistance, mapping a plain file in and
relying on tmpfs or its moral equivalent to get it in and out of swap.
The code will have to have very weird needs and be written by an
uber-geek, but there will be a win.

However, for the vast majority of cases, the well-understood
general-purpose code that has been beaten on by hot kernel people for
years will out-perform, and more importantly be more reliable than,
someone's special hacks.

If someone says `that general purpose stuff is OK for everyone else, but
I have _special_ _needs_', they're almost certainly deluding
themselves.  In 1998 we rarely hear the cry of people who claim they can
hack assembler to be better than the output of a good optimising
compiler; let us hope that tweaky memory management will go the same
way.

ian
