In article <6hiacd$3a1$1@ftel.ftel.co.uk> Ian G Batten <I.G.Batten@batten.eu.org> writes:
>
>  In 1998 we rarely hear the cry of people who claim they can
>hack assembler to be better than the output of a good optimising
>compiler.

  If you so rarely hear it, it's because it goes without saying..
An excellent assembly hacker can often write better code than a
good optimizing compiler (albeit not always; optimizing compilers
do *really* well with some code).

>let us hope that tweaky memory management will go the same way.

  Tweaky memory management is Bad[tm] for the same reason assembly
hacking is bad: it tends to be unportable, less flexible (ie, hard
to change the code when the demands on the application change), 
and quickly obsoleted.  Assembly code that's been well-optimized
for the 386 runs like a dog on a Pentium, for instance.  Similarly,
memory management hacks tend to lose their value when the memory
subsystem of the platform changes.

  We still do them both, though.  I doubt assembly hacking or 
memory-micromanagement will ever fully lose their appeal.

  -- Bill Moyer




