>
>Providing you persuade customers to swallow the
>costs of the additional complexity of managing
>a cluster.
>
>Providing you persuade customers to swallow the
>costs of re-architecting their database to make
>best use of the cluster (partitioning databases)
>etc.
>
>Until you do this you will always get trashed by
>a faster or as fast single system.
>

I don't know what Unix vendors immitation of a cluster you are used to 
however these remarks show you know nothing about clusters.

1) A cluster ( VMS style ) is usually configured as a single management domain.
   It is practically as simple to manage as a single system. 
  
   One system disk.
   One User authorization file.
   Clusterwide installation of most software ( some thirdparties who don't know
   enougth about clustering may require you too install on more than one
   node but 99% install clusterwide).
   Clusterwide print and batch queues.
   Clusterwide system startup file.
   Clusterwide safe access to all files and disks via distributed lock manager.
   Clusterwide mail access and mail notification.

   Practically the only things that need to be done from individual nodes are
   :-    

     Setting up TCP/IP    
   and
     Tuning the system.

   Big deal - you run UCX$CONFIG for the first and Autogen for the second.


2) Database vendors such as Oracle have already done all the work you need to 
   support clustering and this does not involve 'partitioning databases'.
   The database is just as you would set it up for a single node.
   The only difference is that each system has to startup its own instance 
   to access the database - this has it's own rollback segments and redo logs.
   However the instances on the two or more clustered systems access exactly
   the same database files and use the distributed lock manager to co-ordinate
   this access.
 
3) As to cost - this is a tradeoff against disaster tolerance.
   You can either cluster together a number of less powerful systems 
   whose total capacity is just what you need. In which case if you lose a
   system you will continue to function but will not be able to handle your
   full normal transaction load or you spend more money so that you have
   extra capacity which means that you carry on as normal even when you 
   have lost a system.

4) Also if you need extra capacity you can just add extra nodes into the
   cluster.

5) Uptime is also improved since ( assuming only a mirrored system disk ) 
   you can perform rolling operating system upgrades ( ie upgrade the operating
   system with the cluster being available the whole time ).
   As well as having the cluster available the whole time while you take out
   one system and upgrade it's memory , Io sub-systems etc.  
   A couple of years ago I did an upgrade from VAX VMS to Alpha VMS in a
   cluster with the cluster available the whole time ie change of operating
   system version and hardware achitecture. 

Now if clusters are so bad why have Unix vendors been trying to get clustering
working for years and why is clustering all the talk of the WNT people.


Now all of the above applies to VMS clusters. Unfortunately I have never used
Digital Unix's clustering ( we don't have any Digital unix boxes here )
hence I do not know how much of this functionality is in Digital Unix.
I suspect it may not be much better than some of the other "Unix Clustering"
technologies.

Galaxies on the otherhand are not clusters. A Galaxy machine will have multiple
copies of the operating system running on multiple CPUs in the same box.
In some ways this is similar to a cluster with the systems memory acting as the
cluster interconnect. Galaxies are already here - they have been setup and are
running on 8400s at a number of Digital sites. However the real benefit will
come when you can have much larger machines with more memory and cpus.
This is where wildfire comes in - but at the moment this is still vapourware.
However if it comes off then Sun and a number of other vendors will need to
lookout. A 72 Cpu wildfire with 200GB odd of memory with the SMP CPU usability
scaling practically linearly because of the galaxy technology will kick some
arse.
I know - noone will ever buy it - why would you need that power ?
Now where have I heard that before ?

Could it be :-

A 64 bit operating system - why would you need that ?

Well Oracle seemed to show that access to a large memory via 64 bits has this
way of improving performance.

Now who is developing 64 bit operating systems ?

David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader
Middlesex University



