The Pyxis chipset used on the 164LX motherboard does not support AGP. 
While Digital has often made the "right call" on most of the upcoming
buses, Digital will be there in future designs.

FYI, for the record, actually Digital Semi had unique connector found on
the EB64+ that used the SGS Thompson G364 chipset.  It was the "memory
bus" based 64-bit 33.33 Mhz video frame buffer. Although it was not AGP
bus, per se, but it was real close, and the sucker really screamed. 
Since this was proprietary and left over from the Old Mips days,
Digital  can make this happen.

Look for AGP technology in future 21264 designs. . .

For now, we have to live with the world's fastest processor and a PCI
bus.  BTW, the 164LX supports 64-bit PCI, which is 267MB/sec. whereas
Intel's current AGP bus bandwith which is also at 267MB/sec. 
Unfortunatly, the only Video "cards" that operate 64-bit PCI is from
Hitachi, called the Spherix.  The Spherix contains upto 4 Alpha
processors managing OpenGL.  

