You are clutching very hard at straws.

Sticking to non clustered SPECrate_int95 and SPECrate_fp95
numbers because thats what 99.9% of your customers will
be using we get.

                8400/5/625 with    Sun E6000/336 Mhz with
                14 CPU's           18 CPU's
SPECrate_int95  2143               2291

                84000/5/625 with   Sun E6000/336 Mhz with
                12 CPU's           12 CPU's
SPECrate_fp95   1375               1712

It is interesting that you bring up SPECrate_int/SPECrate_fp
because they illustrate my point beautifully. Why have Digital
published a 14 CPU SPECrate_int number and only a 12
CPU SPECrate_fp number. The answer is easy, SPECrate_int
does not consume much memory bandwidth SPECrate_fp does
and the 8400 backplane does not have anything left.

Addressing the clustering for a moment. SPECrate_fp/int scales
well across clusters it is after all an easy way of avoiding
memory contention. Given this a 6 node E4000 cluster
will give the same SPECrate_fp as a 8 node 8400 cluster
or say 3 x 30 CPU E6000's.

Of course appart from these rather bad examples you seem
to lack any other compelling benchmark results that can be
held up to justify any performance advantage claims that
Digital may make.

Lets list the public benchmarks order of merit for the 8400

TPC-C single system 5th behind HP, SUN, IBM, SGI
TPC-C clustered 3rd behind IBM and Sun
TPC-D 1TB no result, leaders Sun, NCR and IBM
TPC-D 300 GB no result, leaders NCR, HP, Sun, DG, Sequent
TPC-D 100 GB 7th behind NCR, IBM (2 results) DG, Unisys,
and a 4 CPU Sun E450.
SAP/R3 8th behind Sun, HP, SNI, IBM/S390, Sequent, AS/400,
IBM RS/6000
