> In article <353665B3.ED40AC5A@columbus.rr.com>, Troy Baer <tbaer@columbus.rr.com> writes:
> |> The performance of the Alpha for FP intensive code is *very* dependent
> |> on the compiler technology.  I don't know about the DEC compilers, but
> |> the Cray Fortran 90 compiler on OSC's T3E (128 300MHz Alphas -- 600
> |> MFLOPS peak per processor) is lucky to get 100-200 MFLOPS per processor
> |> on real-world code.  By contrast, I've also worked on an SGI Power Onyx
> |> (2 75MHz R8000s -- 100 MFLOPS peak per processor), and with the SGI
> |> compilers we got *very* close to peak (~90-95 MFLOPS per processor) with
> |> the exact same Fortran code.
> 
> A 75MHz R8000 can do two mul-adds per cycle - that's 300 MFLOPS peak.

Duh.  You're right, of course.  My 100 MFLOPS/processor number was from
the Linpack 100x100 benchmark, which I shouldn't have described as
peak.  Just so we're talking apples and apples here, I checked the
Netlib performance database server (which I should've looked at before):

http://performance.netlib.org/performance/html/PDSbrowse.html

It looks like the 300MHz Alpha EV5 (the chip in OSC's T3E) clocks in at
140 MFLOPS, which is a far cry from the 600 MFLOPS peak that Cray and
DEC are fond of pointing at.  The MIPS chips may be a little slower, but
they seem to do better at delivering real world performance that's at
least in the same ballpark at their peak performance.

