:    time when the total computer market was expanding at an incredible rate.

These figures are a reflection of a reduction in per MIPS cost for
mainframes by at least a factor of ten and reductions in the cost
for licensing mainframe software.  It is not an indicator
of companies not buying mainframes.  

:    Sun's share of the Unix workstation market might be growing but in the big picture
:    people are buying NT systems to do classical Unix workstation type of work.  If you
:    include the entire workstation market Sun's share is shrinking.

I have a difficult time believing this is true.  Just what  classical 
UNIX type workstation work is being replaced?  Maybe the financial
houses are replacing UNIX workstations, but I don't see much of a real
movement in the engineering and scientific arenas.  I suspect 
a lot of the NT workstation purchases are by people who are already
using a PC to do those tasks buying these new machines which
are actually being classified as workstations.

Rice for one has expanded its use of Sun workstations for the use
of students over the last few years.  Probably the only limiting
factor for adding new systems is finding space for new labs.  UNIX
provides a much better environment for supporting multiple
users who don't have assigned stations across an entire campus,
and Rice has taken advantage of that capability pretty well.



