This is the only way that you have a chance of connecting CDROMs to a
VAX/VMS system that old.  And CDroms are pretty much the only way that you
can get new versions of any software, including VMS.  For you to access the
CDroms on the Infoserver, you do need the Infoserver drivers for the VMS
system.  I think that there was a kit that supported one of the V5 releases.
Maybe V5.4, but I'm not sure.  To access general CDROMs, you would need the
ISO9660 filesystem support, but I think that required one of the V6
releases.

There should be a CDROM with the Infoserver software on it.

If all else fails, you can use the CDROM drives on a PC so something
similar.  They are basically a standard Sony drive with a jumper set to
enable 512 byte sectors by default.  Open up the drives and clip the wire
that loops back to the drive on the cable to the rear unit select and
connect it to a PC and you should be ok.  Unless the mounting for the
optical system is has dried out and it won't flex to track.  Its only 1X, if
you're using a 785, then your PCs must be 286s ;-)

