Paul DeMone <PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net> writes:

>   This is just history repeating itself.  Think back roughly 15 years when
>   Motorola and National were shipping 32 bit processors (68020 and 32032
>   respectively).  The best Intel had to offer was the 16 bit 286.  Same FUD
>   spewed forth about how 32 bit processors were so wasteful and how 99% of
>   programs ran fine with 16 bit ints, segmentation was next to godliness,
>   and flat address spaces were for the simple minded.  Once the 386 shipped
>   the greek chorus stopped moaning immediately.

I don't think it's quite the same thing.

16 bits weren't enough for the majority of programs or advanced operating
systems back then.  32 bits are still sufficient for 99.99% of programs.
And using segmentation for the remainder is awkward, even though it's
possible.

You should also bear in mind that there was an almost direct relation ship
between native word sizes and bus widths; Intel processors suffered
serious lack of bandwidth because of that.  Intel, of course, didn't
care and even shipped 32 bit micros with 16 bit bus.  At the same time,
other 32 bit micros were using 64 bit busses.

>   Once merced is sampling Intel will be trumpeting the benefit of 64 bit
>   architectures near and far and acting as if it was brand new and they
>   invented it.

That I don't doubt.  While I'm sure Sun will tout 64 bit Solaris as an
important new feature, as it is, I somehow doubt they'll claim to have
invented it, considering thet IRIX, DU, AIX, HP-UX (in that order)
are already shipping as 64 bit releases.  Of course, marketing focus
will shift from "you don't need 64 bit" to "we have 64 bit now too".

And I still don't think they're wrong in claiming that most people
don't need 64 bit address spaces.

Casper
