: serious lack of bandwidth because of that.  Intel, of course, didn't
: care and even shipped 32 bit micros with 16 bit bus.  At the same time,
: other 32 bit micros were using 64 bit busses.

Exactly.  Consider that going from 16 bits to 32 bits to 64 bits
involves squaring capacity.  The first shift represents 16 doubling
periods or 8 quadrupling periods.  The second shift represents 32 doubling
periods and 16 quadrupling periods.  The industry hasn't gone through
16 doubling periods yet since the introduction of the first 32 bit
processors, so DRAM capacity hasn't caught up yet.  In addition
most users are well below the 4GB limit in RAM. 

The key factor is that code size and data size aren't growing at
the same rate as processor capacity or even memory capacity, so
you don't need a 64 bit address space to serve most users needs.
The only benefit a 64 bit processor will bring for most people
is the increased integer size for ALU ops, which could be
useful for graphics programs and programs that push a lot
of data around.



