Subject: Summary -- Alpha 3000/300 and 32MB SIMMs
Date: 1998/04/18
Message-ID: <ALDERSON.98Apr18121014@netcom.netcom.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 345435520
Sender: alderson@netcom.netcom.com
Organization: NETCOM On-line services
Reply-To: alderson@netcom.com
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


A few weeks back I asked on this newsgroup and on vmsnet.alpha about a problem
we had with installing 32MB SIMMs into a Model 300.  We got very helpful advise
from a gent in Australia, who forwarded the information that both Alpha and
Sparcstations have problems with 60ns SIMMs, and that we ought to be using 70ns
SIMMs instead.

That information was correct:  The 60ns parts respond *too quickly* to a test
by the firmware, and so are interpreted as 8MB.  After obtaining 70ns SIMMs, we
got better results from the firmware.

However, if we try running with more than 64MB in the box--either 8x8MB or
2x32MB--we crash with a machine check.  (The more memory, the quicker the
crash.)  We updated the firmware to the latest and greatest, which helped out a
bit (5-10 minutes to crash with 192MB, instead of 30 seconds), but we still
crash.  Field Service suggests a motherboard replacement; we're thinking, we're
thinking...

Anyway, I had promised a summary of any e-mail responses.  There you have it.
If anyone out there happens to have seen similar crashes and wants to chime in
with advice, we'd be happy to listen.
