  Immediate need is not the point.  This type of shortsightedness which
  favours the minimum requirements for the task of the hour has brought
  us to where we are now with the Y2K bug.  There are many good reasons to
  have 64 bit addressing even if your programs don't require more than one
  or two GB of address space today.  The ability to perform extensive file
  system memory mapping and capability implementation as sparse addresses
  are two that come to mind immediately. But IMHO the most important is simply
  to get an early development start on where general purpose computation is
  inevitably heading and avoiding the crunch when 32 bit apps start failing
  in production environments or convoluted task partitioning is required
  which introduces errors and rework.

> 
> You should also bear in mind that there was an almost direct relation ship
> between native word sizes and bus widths; Intel processors suffered

  Not true.  Bus width is an implementation cost and compatability issue.  The
  32 bit S/360 had implementations with bus widths from 8 to 64 bits.  The 68K
  ISA had implementations of 8, 16 and 32 bits.  The x86 IA-32 has had impl-
  ementations with 16, 32, 64 bits (and 128 bits when merced ships).  Some
  experimental machines had 16 bit ISAs implemented with 128 bit wide buses.

> serious lack of bandwidth because of that.  Intel, of course, didn't
> care and even shipped 32 bit micros with 16 bit bus.  At the same time,
