Jeffrey J. Potoff wrote:
> Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> > Your grasp of recent processor history seems somewhat poor.but no matter. What you are
> > ignoring completely is that
> > CPU performance isn't worth a candle if you havn't got a
> > balanced system to put them in. Little things like memory
> > latency/bandwidth and I/O latency and bandwidth can be
> > much more important.
> >
> Ah, the Sun mating call.  The bandwidth, oh the bandwidth.  Suppose your
> work is all computational, little I/O and no graphics.  Would you
> pay extra for this 'balanced' system ?  I wouldn't and don't.  There may
> be
> better web or file servers, but the Alphas are the perfect CPU for heavy
> computational use.

The performance of the Alpha for FP intensive code is *very* dependent
on the compiler technology.  I don't know about the DEC compilers, but
the Cray Fortran 90 compiler on OSC's T3E (128 300MHz Alphas -- 600
MFLOPS peak per processor) is lucky to get 100-200 MFLOPS per processor
on real-world code.  By contrast, I've also worked on an SGI Power Onyx
(2 75MHz R8000s -- 100 MFLOPS peak per processor), and with the SGI
compilers we got *very* close to peak (~90-95 MFLOPS per processor) with
the exact same Fortran code.

The Alpha is nice because it has a very fast clock rate, but it's also
somewhat limited by the small on-chip caches (16kB L1 cache [8k data, 8k
instructions] and 96kB L2 cache).  This forces your compilers to be a
lot smarter to get peak performance.

