Indeed.  time_t is typedef'd to be a long on the oldest system I have to
hand (a Unisoft System V.2 machine) and it's typedef'd to be a long on
the newest system I have to hand (Solaris 2.7).  

$ uname -a
UniPlus+ fulcis 5.2V2.5 1274V M68K020
$ grep time_t /usr/include/sys/types.h
typedef long            time_t;

penelope:/u/igb 14:40:09 (517)
$ uname -a 
SunOS penelope.ftel.co.uk 5.7 Beta sun4u sparc SUNW,Ultra-1
penelope:/u/igb 14:40:12 (518)
$ grep time_t /usr/include/sys/types.h
typedef long            time_t; /* time of day in seconds */
penelope:/u/igb 14:40:15 (519)

That being the case, given the ILP32 vs LP64 models coming from the
Colorado 64-bit agreement, surely the only reasonable value for the size
of a time_t is 64 bits?  It's a long.  It always has been a long.  On a
64 bit machine, a long is 64 bits.  What else is there to discuss?

And if anyone's managed to write code that _relies_ on time_t being 32
bits, I'd be interested to see it...

ian
