> 
> Your perspective is very limited.  One HUGE benefit to U-IIi is 100%
> binary compatibility with the rest of Sun's products.

  So? The NVAX chip was 100% binary compatible with the sea of VAXen that
  came before it.  Where is it now?

> 
> In addition to Ultra workstations, I use Intel machines all the time -
> both NT and Solaris x86.  There is nothing in the Intel world that can
> hold a flame to Ultra workstations for reliability or ease of installation.

  So your top three reasons for staying with SPARC is 1) software installed
  base, 2) reliability, and 3) ease of installation.  Well 1 and 2 didn't
  prevent huge slides in the S/390 and VAX markets and 3 hasn't done much
  for the Mac lately.

> 
> You also forget that U-IIi is going to service a large embedded market.

  Old news; companies having been marketing SPARC here for years with little to
  show for it. The industrial computer market (VME and CPCI) in the post 68K era
  seems to be largely falling in line behind PowerPC with a smattering of x86
  for the NTophiles.  Given its ever diminishing presence on the desktop, Motorola
  is unlikely to give up an iota of VME market share without a fierce and bloody
  fight.

> 
> Remember that "economies of scale" doesn't necessarily equate to
> "value".  GM builds the most cars; do we all drive GM cars?

  GM's unit volume lead in cars is quite fragile and by most auto business metrics
  (profit per car, hours labour per car, parts outsourcing, pension liability) GM
  is seriously behind most of its competitors.

  Intel sold about 75 to 80 million CPUs last year compared to what, maybe half a
  million desktop SPARCs.  Given the huge volume disparity, your comparison to GM's
  ranking in the auto industry is most disingenous.
 
> Ultra will be competitive with Alpha soon.  U5 & U10 do leverage PC
> parts infrastructure.  Sun has multiple sourced SPARC processors for a long
> time.  And, much more importantly, Ultra is extremely successful.

  As a profitable business, SPARC has been successful.  In comparison to the
  performance of other big iron RISC CPUs like Alpha, HP, and MIPS,  SPARC has
  consistently trailed behind the pack (often well behind).  And for integer
  performance, SPARC has spent more time trailing Intel's flagship offering
  than not.

> 
> >   As far as Sun's run of good business pushing SPARC and Unix into the server
> >   market (and the resultant profitability and market valuation) goes,  I am
> >   struck by the remarkable similarity to DEC at the height of its success with
> >   VAX and VMS.  But like the VAX, the signs of the downward slide in SPARC will
> >   first be seen in the desktop/workstation segment.  Didn't Sun actually ship
> >   fewer desktop systems last year than the year before?  Sun can keep climbing
> >   to higher and higher branches in the face of the rising Intel waters but
> >   eventually it will run out of tree.
> 
> Shipments were about the same.  This year I'm sure they will grow
> substantially thanks to the U5 and U10.  If you want to look for

  Selling replacements and upgrades to the masochistic remains of the MicroSPARC
  installed base is hardly a growth industry.

> troubled Unix desktop vendors you best be looking at all the others
> who have caved in to the pressure from Sun & Intel.  While they
> have all decided to try Merced it seems rather stupid when even Intel
> says Merced isn't for desktops.  I suspect Sun's workstation sales

  Do you think Intel is so stupid as to pull an Osborne?  Merced is only for
  servers - yah right.  Intel said the same thing for every chip since the
  486.  Given the increasingly competent x86 competition, Intel will drive
  Merced down the product line as fast as possible.  One of the trade rags
  even reported that Intel has a team working on a Merced derivative for
  mobile applications.

> are going to do very well absorbing all the business that the other
> vendors are throwing away.
> 
> It is really amazing how people have put so much faith in Merced -
> a totally unproven technology.  Even if they get the silicon right,
> which is a huge job in itself, they are going to need a few
> miracles in compiler technology.

  You are correct in your disgust for the manner in which a vap-o-chip,
  years from first silicon, soiled the shorts of quite a few RISC vendors.

  However, it is hardly unproven technology, EPIC is basically RISC with a
  twist.  For an introduction to very wide issue, fully predicated computer
  architectures, and the compiler technology needed to exploit them, please
  read "Compiler Technology for Future Microprocessors" by W.W. Hwu et al in
  the Dec 1995 Proceedings of the IEEE.  Merced will come, and by combining
  architectural advances with Intel's design and process capabilities, it
  will be *fast*. 

