On 14 Apr 1998 23:52:45 GMT, Joseph F. Bergevin
<jfberge@rs6000.cmp.ilstu.edu> wrote:

>I got a hold of an AXP-PCI/33 MB (AT compatible) with a 21066 (I believe) chip.
>The manual says that it requires true parity RAM. I only had Non-parity laying
>around, so I tried it. I got nothing. Not a beep, not a BIOS video message, not
>an LCD error code. The board is new (I just opened the box) and I know that the
>power supply and misc components work. The board came with 512K of cache
>preinstalled. My questions is, then, do these boards present themselves as dead
>to the world UNLESS they have true parity RAM? I don't wanna invest more money
>in some parity RAM if it's likely that my board is just toast.
>Thanks...

I don't know how they present without parity RAM, but my guess would
be not very well.  Yes, they do REQUIRE true parity RAM (NOT logic
parity) because they do ECC on the processor for all RAM accesses.  So
it's not too farfetched to assume that you wouldn't have enough data
lines from RAM to processor and the processor would assume that there
was no RAM installed.  this could very well lead to your blank screen
situation.

btw, I have one of these boards - you can find true parity ram pretty
cheap and they don't make an especially bad workstation (not extremely
fast or anything, but most definitely useable).

Jon



