Terry Shannon wrote:

> Butch wrote:
> >
> > I think that, between Compaq's marketing muscle and some of the agreements
> > that have already been in place, Compaq will be a force to deal with by the
> > other computer firms. Their Alpha chip is well along in the 64 bit arena as
> > far as solutions go compared to the others.
> >
>
> Yup. Digital UNIX may not have half as many apps as Solaris, but they're
> all 64-bit apps. And with another vendor joining in Tandem with Sequent
> and DEC, apps ought to be easier to come by.
>

Yes all Alpha apps are 64 bit, do all Alpha apps use the64 bit features  of the
Alpha like >4GB memory addressing
no. Most are simply 32 bit  apps ported to Alpha with no
additional work done to enhance the app.

Does this give DU/Merced some sort of kick start, no. ISV's
will treat each  platform DU/Intel, DU/Alpha as two separate
versions with two separate  business cases. DU/Alpha and
DU/Intel will have to stand separately on  their own strengths
and they aren't great.

Anyone who doubts this should remember that Digital has
been unable to capture more than 25% of the available NT
ISV's and applications to the NT/Alpha platform.

Tandem/Sequent are hardly a force in the UNIX market and
Digital is in a simular position. Gartner lists them all as second
or third tier vendors, these are not vendors who are going to
excite ISV's into supporting their platforms.

Remember it does not matter that DU/Alpha and DU/Intel are
code compatible, since porting from one platform to another is
not the major cost to an ISV. ISV's are more concerned about
support and testing costs which are not diminished by having
code compatibility.

It is also worth pointing out that if you add Digitals, Sequents
and Tandems UNIX systems revenue together you end up
with an entity that is still firmly in 4th place in the UNIX
market behind Sun, HP and IBM and with SGI snapping
at their heals.

