>
>Terry Shannon wrote:
>>
>> Yup. Digital UNIX may not have half as many apps as Solaris, but they're
>> all 64-bit apps. And with another vendor joining in Tandem with Sequent
>> and DEC, apps ought to be easier to come by.
>
>Yes all Alpha apps are 64 bit, do all Alpha apps use the
>64 bit features of the Alpha like >4GB memory addressing?
>no.  Most are simply 32 bit apps ported to Alpha with no
>additional work done to enhance the app.

  Many applications rely heavily on library calls, and the
standard C library *has* been well-optimized on the Alpha, 
so even a simple recompile can turn a strictly 32-bit app 
into a 64-bit app.  Any application that that spends signi-
ficant time on memory allocation, block data transfer, or 
string manipulation will see a tremendous performance delta 
when recompiled on the Alpha (potentially far better than 
8x for things like strcmp(), since not only can the Alpha 
operate on strings 8 bytes at a time per inner-loop itera-
tion, but the CMPBGE instruction does in one clock what in 
other processors requires several instructions).

  It's ironic to me that conventional wisdom says that the
slower-clocked x86 and PPC are "as powerful" as the Alpha 
because they "do more" per clock cycle, when for many kinds
of algorithms the opposite is true.  CMPBGE screams through
string manipulations, while MULQ + UMULH performs the same
operation as four multiplies + four adds + some branching 
(and on the register-starved x86, probably some hits to the
memory bus as well) on the 32-bit systems.  

  The combination of a powerful instruction set and the 
fastest clock on the planet makes the Alpha serious fun 
to program, and serious big iron for tackling the biggest
number/text-grinding jobs.

>Anyone who doubts this should remember that Digital has
>been unable to capture more than 25% of the available NT
>ISV's and applications to the NT/Alpha platform.

  This was DEC stealth-marketing at work.  I don't want 
to add to the chorus of "change is in the wind, baa baa"
but I am *very* interested in seeing what will happen 
now that marketing the Alpha has fallen to Compaq and 
Samsung's shoulders.

  My compile job's finally completed -- gotta go.

  -- Bill Moyer




