wayned@cpcug.org (Wayne Dernoncourt) writes:

>I got a phone call last week from a guy in another department asking
>me for help with one of their systems.  It tunrs out that this is a
>Sun 3/75 or Sun 3/160 (this according to the boot-up screen banner).
>There is a dual ribbon cable coming out of the back of the Sun cabinet
>and going to another box that is some sort of scanner for reading and
>storing x-ray films.  In looking through the documentation, there is
>a LSI-11 (something) with a DR-11 running the film scanner.

>When I boot the system, it finds the disk drive, mounts it, etc. but
>seems to have problems with the film scanner.  It seems to hang a long
>time on scanning the SCSI bus, but then again that might be because of
>the external optical drives(?). At the end of this, it dismounts the
>disk and says to repair the disk manually and then restart with
>/etc/something.  One of things I need to have Frank do is put in an
>optical disk and try rebooting.

The exact messages would be very helpful. Which disk? Which
/etc/something?

If your device is indeed using DR11, the SCSI bus does not come
into play. Or the DR11 does not come into play when the SCSI bus
is active. *Unless* you DR11 is screwing the VMEbus. Try removing
the board with the dual ribbon cables when power is down. Then
try booting.

>According to Frank, one day it just wouldn't boot, so it doesn't sound
>like a disk has to be in there.  It's not clear if the DR-11 in the
>scanner isn't responding, if the Sun equipment is having a problem or
>if the LSI is out to lunch.  The optical drives are powered on, but
>I'm not sure if there is anything in them.

Would be easdier to diagnose if I had more and better info, sorry.
The DR11 communications coule be started during boot-up, but probably
*after* the disk check. So I suppose one of your disk drives has gone
south or so.

>BTW, they first called the company that sold it to us and asked for help.
>They said sure we'll fix it for $10k.  In case you were wondering, this
>system is 3 or 4 years old.

I can't believe this system is only 3 to 4 years old. 3/anything was
long out of production then. I can believe 13 years, though. Roughly
speaking, the 3/75 or 3/160 were the current machines then. Please
look at the banner the machine displays before booting again. Also,
please describe the machine physically. A 3/75 is a desktop machine
with a large (by today's standards) footprint. The monitor is
on top of a desktop box. Looking at the back of the box, you should
see two slots. The top one is a single VMEbus slot that normally
holds a SCSI controller. A DR11 is quite unlikely here. The machine
would be diskless if it had a DR11.

The 3/160 is a deskside machine with 10 (?) VMEbus slots. The first
slot has the CPU board (leftmost looking at the back). SCSI should
be in slot eight or so, and is probably only internal. The disk(s)
and tape drive are in the top of the box. You should be able to
detach the front cover, then remove a few screws, then lower the
internal front on hinges. Be careful if you ever do this, the
internal front carries a heavy power supply. When you do this,
you should have access to the drive compartment in the top.
I believe you have to remove a few more screws, then slide out
a tray that carries the drives. If a drive is gone, you will
be able to replace it with something that fits a 5 1/4" form
factor. Good luck locating such a device... Also with restoring
the machine.
