We did indeen produce a VAXServer 3100. This was available as either a
Model 10 or Model 20 variation of the MV3100.
Yes, what you have is a MV3100, a model 10 if it is a short cabinet and a
model 20 if it is a high cabinet. The tow are easily distingushable from
each other, the model 10 could have 1 x 5.25 inch and 1 x 3.5 inch SCSI
device and the Model 20 had an upper drive shelf, so you could have 3 x
3.5 inch devices on the upper shelf and 2 x 5.25 inch devices on the lower
shelf.
The VAXServer was sold, pre-loaded with OpenVMS and PATHWORKS whereas the
MV3100 was sold pre-loaded with OpenVMS only. The idea was that for those
users requiring a PATHWORKS server, a small two user OpenVMS License was
sufficient to administrate the OpenVMS and PATHWORKS, and then licensing
for PC use was done via PATHWORKS itself.

There is a very big difference between the VAXStation 3100 product set and
MV3100 product set. The requisite hardware required to interface into a
Graphics Controller was never built into a MV3100. Instead, we added a
second SCSI bus. Thus in the MV3100 model 10 and 20, the is a SCSI-A
internal bus and a SCSI-B external bus, the 50 pin connector you mention.

The DB25 connector is in fact a fully functional serial port.

For a while, I wasa the Product Engineer in Manufacturing for this product
while it was produced in Kanata, as well as variations on the theme, such
as the InfoServer 100 and InfoServer 150.

For those that really want to know, the MV3100 design did evolve from the
VAXStation 3100 design, after all, why re-invent the wheel when in fact a
good CAD program can help you with Cut and Paste.

You can license the VAXServer 3100 as you would a MV3100 and all will be well.

rtt

