In article <6gs58d$ff9@bgtnsc03.worldnet.att.net>, "John Furcht" <johnfurcht@worldnet.att.net> writes:
:mounting a new tk-50 tape, I received a write lock error. I tried this on
:another new tape, and
:old tapes I have with the same results. What causes this to happen? I cannot
:backup to the tape
:with this error.
:The slide on the cartridge is in the write position...

  If the orange is not visible in the window and the slider is pushed
  to the outside edge of the cassette and away from the middle, then
  the cartridge is (should be) writeable.

  If the cartridge was originally written on a TK70 or higher density
  drive, then it will not be writeable on a TK50 drive.  (Sometimes
  one sees a bulk degausser used here...)

  Alternatively, the TK50 drive might be having problems correctly
  detecting or processing the setting of the write protect slot on
  the side of the cassette.  (This slot is how the slider can tell
  the drive the cassette is writeable or non-writeable.)

  It is also possible that an unload and reload was not performed.
  (When one uses DISMOUNT without /NOUNLOAD, one must physically
  remove and reinsert the cartridge, as the default behaviour of
  /UNLOAD will deliberately simulate the requirement for operator
  intervention with the drive before it will allow a MOUNT...)

  In other words, if DISMOUNT/NOUNLOAD is not specified, or if the
  default is not altered via MOUNT/NOUNLOAD, one must remove and
  reinsert the cartridge before a subsequent MOUNT will be accepted.

:Microvms 4.7, command: MOUNT MUA0: /FOR

  Whoa, that's truely old software...  V7.1 is current...

