In article <6ggkas$62a$1@news01.deltanet.com>, "Phlip" <tegan@deltanet.com> writes:
:If I have a question about Windows NuT or TCP/IP, I can typically type it
:into DejaNews and find hundreds of discussions about it.
:
:But if the question is about VAX I typically get a rather thin hit count.

  You might want to start with more appropriate search strings, strings
  such as "VMS" and "OpenVMS".  "VAX" and "Alpha" are the hardware platforms
  upon which OpenVMS (formerly known as "VMS") runs, and you are clearly
  seeking software information...  There are extensive archives of OpenVMS
  information available via INFO-VAX and at http://www.dejanews.com/.

:You may have noticed my recent screaming pleading posts to these newsgroups
:about some rather obscure and obsolete VAX technologies. I have suspected
:the discussions went somewhere else.

  I have not noticed your earlier questions.

  General guidelines I have provided to folks considering posting:

    o I would humbly suggest the avoidance of potentially incendiary
      phrasing when asking questions.  (I'm not sure what you meant
      by "screaming pleading", but I didn't see a "smilie"...)
    o I would also humbly suggest making an attempt to answer the
      question from the various available sources before posting
      -- not the least of which is the documentation.  (Folks do
      tend to tire of answering the same questions repeatedly...)
    o I would also suggest posting relevent product information
      and version numbers when asking the question.

:Now I learn that DejaNews is sparse for VAX because the discussions happen
:in a system called "VAX Notes".

  "VAX Notes" is the old name of a conferencing package available
  for OpenVMS systems -- the package is now known as DEC Notes.
  A number of sites have Notes conferences on their OpenVMS systems,
  and gateways or clients for non-OpenVMS systems.  Most of these
  notes conferences are behind firewalls or gateways, and are not
  available to internet users.  Some are...

  Notes provides a permanent archive of postings, and uses a single
  OpenVMS file to contain the entire discussion.  Locally, one can
  easily retrieve Notes conference discussions going back a decade
  or more...  There are a number of similarities to netnews, but
  there are a couple of big differences, too -- the centralized,
  monolithic file structure of Notes is one obvious difference.

:How do I browse this thing? How do I search its archives? Does it have a Web
:page (yet ;-)?

  Yes, there are web gateways available for various Notes conferences
  -- the folks at DECUS have a gateway available for specific DECUS
  notes conferences.  (The Notes conference gateway is accessable via
  the URL http://eisner.decus.org/.)

  As for search engines, I am not aware of any generally-available
  search engines that will query notes conferences, though I have seen
  search engines that can be extended to search notes conferences.
  (As most sites tend to keep Notes conferences private, relatively
  few have looked to provide general access to the conferences via
  Internet search engines.)  (Notes itself supports a basic search
  command.)

	--

  You will also want to avail yourself of the OpenVMS Frequently Asked
  Questions (FAQ) (and INFO-VAX), with information available at the URL:

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms

  You may also want to avail yourself of the resources of organizations
  such as DECUS, such as DECUServe.  The primary DECUS website URL is
  http://www.decus.org/.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.ZZenet.dec.com
  note to those folks not contributing spam -- there is no ZZ in my address




