In article <3530ee83.3224329670@news.jps.net>, SpamTrap@[127.0.0.1] writes:
>	I have a VaxSERVER (NOT a VAXStation -- this is definitely a
>VaxSERVER) 3100 system that I could really use some hardware info on.

Try to boot a VAXstation 3100 without a keyboard and it says it is a
VAXserver 3100 and cries for another license...

>	I'm pretty sure that, at least where booting and CPU is concerned, it
>is pretty much the same as its VAXStation 3100 brethren. What I could
>really use is connector purpose/pinout info for the DB50 (is this the
>SCSI-A port?), Centronics-36 (labeled '4-11'), amd the male DB25 which
>bears a symbol I've never seen before on DEC or any other machines.

Seems to be a SCSI-I type (asynchronous 3.5 Mbit/s) or on the newer models
SCSI-II type (synchronous 5 MBit/s) SCSI-A or SCSI-B (depends on the model)
and a V.24 (modem) type interface...

>	Once again... this is a VaxSERVER 3100 -- it is -not- a VAXStation
>3100! Sorry to sound like a broken record, but the last time I asked I got
>tons of responses about the VAXStation unit and none for the VAXServer.

I don't think there are major differences between a VAXstation 3100 and a
VAXserver 3100. There are of course differences between different models
of VAXstation/VAXserver (10/20/30/40/38/48/76) which may be bigger between
VAXstation models than between a VAXstation and a VAXserver. And then there
of course differences between a VAXstation 3100 and a MicroVAX 3100...

