Organization: Trident Computer Resources
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


I have seen several cases of Venturis systems with DE450's that appear 
to be completely dead from the networking point of view.  The systems
behave like there is bad hardware, either the motherboard, the PCI/ISA 
riser card or the DE450.

It turned out to be a software glitch in Windows 95.  Even though all values
shown in the Network Control Panel were correct, the Networking was stone
dead.  Check the network activity light on the DE450 and see if it is
flashing during network activity.  It should blink even with activity 
generated by other nodes on the network.  If it doesn't show activity, you
either have a hardware problem like a bad cable, or bad hardware in the PC, 
or Windows 95 needs to be given a good "kick".

If you can't find anything wrong with the hardware, try going to the Network
Control Panel and removing all of the Networking configuration; adapter
and Client for Microsoft Networks.  (Certainly make sure you have all of 
the configuration data recorded somewhere first!)  Try rebooting and then
putting back the Network configuration.  If that doesn't work, (after all
it is a PC), you might need to actually remove all the Networking 
configuration, shtudown, remove the DE450 hardware from the system, reboot,
shutdown, add back the DE450 hardware, reboot and restore the Network 
configuration.

If you have no other PCI/ISA hardware added to the Venturis, the DE450
should just work without changing an adapter or hardware related Windows
parameters.  You should only need configure your TCP/IP values if your
using TCP/IP. 

Neil Sargent wrote:
> 
> We have a DEC Venturis FX witha DE450 Etherworks Turbo PCI 10 Adapter.
> We've wired the computer to the school's ethernet.  When we start up our
> web browser it connects fine.  However, if we try to browse the schools
> network (to connect to our apple laser printers) we get a message
> stating 'Unable to browse the Network...network is unaccessable'.  We
> don't really have any "Windows" experts here (obviously!).  We've tried
> different 'Client', 'Adapter' and 'Protocol' settings to no avail.  Any
> advice would whole-heartedly appreciated.
> 
> Thank you
> 
> Sincerely
> Neil Sargent



