In article <35299292.B8A@concentric.net>,
Stuart Brook  <sbrook@concentric.net> wrote:
>This will be a *highly* disappointing computer for kids in schools ... 
>There are no graphics other than what you can create with ascii
>characters (and some lines)  and there are few "games" or word
>processors or internet access programs etc etc etc.

If the kinds are prejudiced by Mac/Winblows style computing then I agree
they'll hate it, but if they don't have specific expectations it's possible
for them to have a lot of fun with a PDP-11.  The kids at my high school
wrote their own Breakout, Space Invaders, Snake (started out as Centipede but
mutated), and adventure-type games, and a bunch of others I don't remember.
VT100 terminals are limiting but with some imagination you can have fun
with them, we later got a couple of GIGIs (low-end color graphics terminals)
and were able to do a lot more stuff with those.  It's easy to write
multi-user games on a PDP-11 (w/o having to know about network programming),
and the 11 is as good as anything else for more serious stuff like learning
programming languages, I suppose these days printing your term paper in a
monospace font with no descenders isn't good enough but it can do simple
things like that.

John Wilson
