  Yes, among the items, you are missing the choice of operating system,
  and the communications interconnect.  I could forsee at least OpenVMS,
  UNIX, Linux, and Windows NT working here...  (I have seen INNs, POP3,
  webservers, and SMTP transports for everything other than Windows NT,
  and I would be surprised to learn that these packages were not also
  available for Windows NT.  Though I am guessing the Radius server is
  Windows NT?)

  And you might also want to consider if you are willing to purchase
  used equipment.  (I'd tend not "go older" than the AlphaStation and
  AlphaServer series, given the availability of PCI and similar widgets.
  Older Alpha systems did not have PCI.  I'd expect you would be able
  to find a good deal on a used AlphaServer 1000 4/266 series. :-)

	--

  In this price space, I would look at the DIGITAL Personal Workstation
  (DPWS).  For OpenVMS, Windows NT, and UNIX, look at the -au series. 
  For (only) Windows NT, look at the -a series.  (Check for operating
  system software licensing restrictions that might apply -- Windows NT
  Workstation has/had a restriction on the number of webserver clients
  permitted, for instance. I seem to recall it was 10?)

  The other obvious platform option here would be the AlphaServer 300.
  One can add an additional SCSI controller and other options to the
  AlphaServer 300.  (I'd guess the single SCSI on the DPWS can still
  likely hold more storage than you probably need.)

