Ga'ash Soffer wrote:
> 
> that is suprising
> a 702mhz PII should be able to kill most alpha systems, at least under
> x86 emulated stuff on NT.

Now why on earth would any fool want to do that? That is like 
trying to use a thoroughbred race horse to pull a hay wagon!
One would hope that you are NOT buying a 64bit 767Mhz Alpha
to run emulated software "just because you can". You buy the 
box to get real work done not to play games on. Most people 
could not tell the difference between a 486 (w 40Mb memory an
W95) and an Alpha (w 128M memory and WNT) running Excel, Word
type applications emulated or otherwise.  You buy the big Alpha
for doing graphics rendering or some other CPU intensive app.
Let's face it, most users don't even use 1/3 of the CPU time on
the 486/586 box they have now.  If you most of the low end boxes 
were configured with sufficient memory, there wouldn't be a "NEED"
for a faster box.  Remember I am talking about 85-90% of the PC's 
and systems sold today.  Most PC's are used for nothing more than
glorified word processors that you can play games on.  Give me a 
Break!  

Until the AT and PCI buses become a lot faster than they are today,
the CPU is the least of our worries.  

The I/O is now the single limiting factor in processing information.
(This includes disks, tapes, cd's, dialup links, etc. etc. etc.)
Unless you are doing a lot of number-crunching or graphics intensive
(rendering or displaying) or WEB serving the average Joe only needs 
 < 300Mhz PII with 40Mb memory.  He would never know the difference
