Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.org.decus,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


Disclaimer: I am not a spokesthing for the SIGs.  I am passionately
interested in revitalising them, and I'm emitting in all directions
to try and engender activity to that end, but I formally represent
only myself and the Security and WWW SIGs.

Jeff Killeen wrote:
> 
> >1. I would like the Board to instruct the staff to plan a campground
> >   in L.A. for any SIG that requests one by such-and-such a date.  No
> >   further approval needed for the basic stuff (room, chairs, tables,
> >   electricity, network - just a 10baseT repeater).
> 
> Would this work if there was a pool of resources that was first come first
> serve?

I think so, although 'resources' is very vague.  If the room/s is/were
large enough, I don't see why multiple SIGs (assuming any want to
participate) couldn't share.  It's been done in the past, sometimes
with successful cross-pollination and sometimes not.  I think that
room for five campgrounds might be enough - but let me ask on the
SIGC list to see how many are interested.

As has been pointed out by someone else, if the Board is *serious*
about trying to revitalise the SIGs, or helping/allowing them to
revitalise themselves, it would be close to key to have the
campground/s in a high-traffic area, and not stuck off in a
corner somewhere.  And when I say 'high-traffic' I mean like
'on the way to/from lunch/the tradeshow/registration/whatever.'
(I think this was Larry Kilgallen's suggestion, in fact, though
I may be wrong.)  And the signage should be attractive and
informative, rather than bald and cryptic.  Maybe something as
simple as 'Campgrounds (Topical discussion/demo areas): this way ->'.

> >3. I would like the User Session CDT, if and when, to keep the SIGs
> >   apprised of its scheduling activities on a regular basis.  This
> >   way any interested SIGs can, if they feel so inclined, do something
> >   to help their members at L.A. (like print up SIG-oriented roadmap
> >   cards, for instance).
> 
> YES! I will check on funding this.  Also would you want some funding for
> identity items?

That would be nice - but nicer still, I think, would be an open
instruction to the DECUS staff that SIG requests are to be given
high (or at least reasonable) priority.  Again, if the Board is
serious about SIG reactivation, this is something I think needs
to be addressed.  In the past support from the staff has often
been on an oh-by-the-way basis - no-one's fault, really; they're
busy enough.  But to get the SIGs on the staff's radar screens,
as you put it, would be a definite plus - if not critical.

Back to the issue of 'identity items.'  A couple of years ago
(maybe even last year) the Board offered funding to the SIGs for
this, but I don't think very much was done with it.  I suspect
one reason for this was fragmentation - it was an offer of which
advantage could only be taken by those who either had contacts
and savvy about getting such stuff (like those who were with
vendors that already had paths for getting giveaways for their
booths), or had plenty of time to develop the contacts.

The DECUS office obviously has such contacts already, since
each Symposium includes DECUS identity items.  Therefore, I
would like the SIGs (again, such as are interested) to be able
to use the DECUS framework already in place.

Don't put a big burden on the staff at all; just have each
interested SIG identify a contact to the staffperson responsible
for the promotional stuff, who can then pass along that info
to the giveaway vendor.  From that point on, it would be up
to the SIG contact and the vendor to figure something out,
and the only other staff involvement would be when the SIG
contact said, 'okey, we've got an order.'

Funding: Somewhere along the way the SIG contacts would have
to be told their budget ceilings. From what I remember when I
did this myself, small plastic items in sufficient quantity
could probably be had for ~US$600; if caps or T-shirts or the
like are involved, the price goes up considerably.  Things like
quick-reference cards or screwdrivers or buttons - I don't
know.

Here's how I think things *could* be worked out, based upon the
above (obviously subject to alteration, since I don't have
all the facts - this is just a strawman):

1. The Board determines a minimum funding level (say, $500 per
   SIG involved - that's what it was last time).
   Deadline: by 1 May 1998.
2. A staff contact is identified for SIG promotional stuff.
   Deadline: by 1 May 1998.
3. The SIGs are notified of the above, and invited to identify
   their contact points to the staff liaison.
   Deadline: by 1 June 1998.
4. The Board determines a budget ceiling based upon the SIG
   participation (those who identified contacts by 1 June 1998).
   Deadline: 15 June 1998.
5. The staff liaison passes the SIG contact info along to the
   DECUS office's promotional vendor.
   Deadline: Within days of being received from the SIGs, and
             no later than 7 June 1998 in any event.
6. The participating SIGs are notified of their spending caps.
   Deadline: 16 June 1998.  (It's just a mail message, after all.)
7. The SIGs work with the vendor to figure out what they want,
   get pricing info, and work up an order.
   Deadline: TBD.
8. The SIGs submit their orders to the staff liaison, who
   approves them (if under the budget amount per SIG) and
   submits them to the vendor.
   Deadline: TBD.

Possible pitfalls I see with this include the logistics of
the staff handling, shipping, keeping separate, and passing
along the SIG items.  However, they already do that to some
extent, so the existing framework should be at least enough
for starters.

