> Now, we've installed a DEC Alphastation 500 with Digital UNIX for working
> with CAD and we want that this workstation be a client for accessing to the
> Windows NT resources.
> My doubt is: �Can Digital Unix access to the servers' resources like we do
> with Windows NT without installing additional software?
> 
> For example:
> 
>  In Windows NT or DOS (with TCP/IP protocols) you can do this to access to a
> disk resource:
> 
> NET USE G: \\200.200.200.10\disk1
> 
> �Is there an alternative command to do the same under Digital UNIX?

Not exactly.  With Samba, you can map drives the other way (ie.
have your files on the uniz machine and map the unix drive on the
NT machine).  Smbclient works like ftp, so you could get files from
NT machines that way.  Otherwise, you're going to need to install an
outboard NFS product on the NT side of things if you want to have files
served by an NT machine.

