>> The Vax II/GPX appears to be complete (except for monitor) and the 3100 was
>> gutted for its harddrives.  Now I know that these things aren't worth that
>> much money anymore, but is it worth my time to do anything with them?
>
>The 3100 is definitely worth it.  Just buy a SCSI hard drive, hook it
>up to a terminal and find somebody to grab VMS from.  I just took the
>Toshiba CD-ROM out of my intel-videogame and it booted from there just
>fine.

Some models of the 3100 can only boot a 1 GB drive.  This limit can be
relaxed in a firmware upgrade to some models, but not others.  Other
disks can be bigger, but the boot code can only handle a 1 GB drive. 

>> I have always wanted to learn VMS but never had a machine to run it on, but
>> is it still worth my while?
...
>> Though you cant, of course, classify VMS as a *dead* operating system, is
>> there much use in "learning" it (its intricacies, programming, etc) for the
>> future?
>
>It gives you quite a different view on how an operating system could be like.

Unless your interest is strictly academic, you are best off studying
what your current or potential employer will pay you to use.

>And later on you can reuse the station for Net/OpenBSD if you really
>feel like it.  Regardless of the OS it could make a really good server
>in your home network.

The last time I looked, BSD did not run on 3100.

>> What characteristics keep VMS so stable?  And how can these be built into
>> another operating system?

A commitment to quality that is endemic to Digital's VMS group and
incomprehensible to most of the rest of the industry.

