Compaq Reportedly Plans to Absorb Digital

 By HIAWATHA BRAY 
 c. c.1998 The Boston Globe 

 Compaq Computer Corp.'s acquisition of Digital Equipment
 Corp. suddenly looks less like an alliance and more like an
 absorption. 

 That's the implication of comments made recently by Digital senior
 vice president Harry Copperman. In an interview this week with PC
 Week magazine, Copperman said Compaq has abandoned its
 intention to run Digital as a wholly owned subsidiary. Instead,
 Copperman said Digital would be fully integrated into the structure of
 Compaq, to make the acquisition work more efficiently. 

 ``The plan right now is not to run two separate companies,''
 Copperman said. ``It was clear after the first meeting ... that there
 were too many opportunities not to integrate.'' 

 Fully integrating Digital and Compaq could bring some benefits, but
 also a new set of challenges for Compaq managers and Digital
 employees _ and, possibly, layoffs for some at Digital _ analysts
 predicted. 

 Thursday, officials at both companies refused to expand on
 Copperman's remarks. They said that they are in a
 government-mandated ``quiet period'' until the acquisition is
 completed. 

 As a subsidiary, Digital could have operated with its own
 management structure and considerable independence in corporate
 strategy. 

 By absorbing Digital, Compaq could add efficiencies by eliminating
 overlapping positions in the two companies, said Andy Zaleta, vice
 president of the global technology practice at A.T. Kearney Executive
 Search in Boston. But Digital, as the company being acquired, would
 likely bear the brunt of any job losses. 

 Digital executives and workers might also find themselves reporting
 to Compaq executives _ and might even be required to move to
 Compaq's headquarters in Houston. 

 ``That could create some possibly big problems, if you're trying to
 force-fit two different kinds of operations,'' Zaleta said.



