If you're not interested/involved in DECUS (the Digital Equipment
Computer Users Group), feel free to skip this.  Well, feel free
to skip it in any event. :-D

Some items came up in a recent thread in comp.org.decus that I think
were getting rather off-topic - so I'm starting a new thread here
to focus the discussion.  Some of the articles in the original
thread were cross-posted to comp.org.decus and comp.os.vms, so
I'm cross-posting this new one to catch the eye of anyone watching
the old one in the other newsgroups.  I'm adding comp.sys.dec,
too, because I hear a lot of DECheads lurk there. ;->

Jeff Killeen wrote in article <6g4n2j$2ng$1@winter.news.erols.com>:
> 
> Ken the Board has tried 4 different time to re-engage the SIGs in major ways
> and in minor ways.  So to answer your question what does this half to do with
> LA until the SIGs can be defined as to what they really are today they will be
> left out of the planning.

Being "left out of the planning" has a rather final sound to it.  It
makes it seem as though the SIGs would be ignored even if they offered
input - and under no circumstances would they even be consulted or invited
to offer or review suggestions.

Now maybe that's not the intent - but here's a factoid: I'm the chair
of two SIGs and run the onsite Web server at the Symposia.  There has
been nothing from the Board (or anyone else) keeping the SIGs informed
or inviting participation.  The only reason there's any discussion
of SIG involvement at L.A. occurring on SIGC (the SIG chair mailing
list) is because I started it.  So this would seem to support that
the Board (or Symposia planners, whomever) isn't about to open the
door on its own - the SIGs need to knock first.

>                            IMO and take it FWIW it is likely nothing the Board
> does is going to resolve this - it really needs to come from the SIGs - the
> SIGs will find any reasonable cost self-contain proposal will be galdly
> accepted by the Board.

Proposals, proposals - we seem to be drowning in process (which may or
may not be appropriate).  While a few proposals (e.g., the Interactive
Sessions) have turned out successfully, how many other worthwhile endeavours
have not been undertaken because of the bugaboo of having to "write a
proposal"?  Speaking for myself, it's daunting.  And I think it's
possibly being taken too far - the latest word I received when I
remarked that I'd like to have a campground at L.A. was "I'll take it
up with the Board."  Board approval is now required for a Symposium
feature that has been around for decades?  This doesn't sound like
SIG empowerment (to use a somewhat overused term) or process streamlining
to me - it sounds like innovation-choking micro-management.  (MHO, which
I'm willing to change given appropriate evidences.)

Gritch, gritch, gritch.  I shouldn't just sling arrows outrageously,
so here are some (hopefully) constructive comments/requests:

1. I would like the Board to instruct the staff to plan a campground
   in L.A. for any SIG that requests one by such-and-such a date.  No
   further approval needed for the basic stuff (room, chairs, tables,
   electricity, network - just a 10baseT repeater).

2. I would like the User Session CDT (content development team), if and
   when it's formed, to be *required* to at least ask the SIGs to
   offer input and suggestions.  It doesn't need to be required (at
   this point) to do anything with whatever it receives - just to
   make the offer.

3. I would like the User Session CDT, if and when, to keep the SIGs
   apprised of its scheduling activities on a regular basis.  This
   way any interested SIGs can, if they feel so inclined, do something
   to help their members at L.A. (like print up SIG-oriented roadmap
   cards, for instance).

There, how's that for *starters*?  I think it can open the door so any
interested SIGs can start "defining" themselves - and these steps
are certainly low-impact if no SIG actually does.  So why not?

#ken	P-|}

