In article <6g1gff$81m$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, murray@online.no writes
>In article <01bd5d1a$74567d40$fe6f02cf@errno.bpc.net>,
>  "Jesse Rehmer" <errno@bpc.net> wrote:
>>
>I would like prices as well, however, for a AlphaServer 800 with:
>
>
>400 Mhz
>128MB
>Large Cache?
>3x 9GB SCSI
>Internet Software Suite

I can't give you any prices, but I've got some limited experience with
using 800's for internet services with IASS.

>Also does anyone know how many of the above configured servers I would need to
>run a startup ISP offering:

First question - you say you're a startup ISP, but what sort of growth
are you expecting ? You could easily run your proposed machines into the
ground if you grow too quickly and don't plan for it ! At least with the
AlphaServers, you've got decent scalability...

>1.news feed

A full news feed would eat your proposed 800's for breakfast and come
back for seconds. The more RAM and disk space, you can throw at it, the
better. I wouldn't run anything like that with less than 512Mb of RAM
and a decent number of disks in a Storageworks cabinet - probably 4 x
9Gb to start with, and make damn sure you keep an eye on the amount of
space used. You'll definitely want to invest in a better disk controller
too, if you want to use RAID.

Remember that current estimates say that a full feed can saturate a T-1
line, so plan accordingly.

>2.support 60 dial up users using 56K tech or 65K ISDN.

A machine for accouting, dialup access and shell accounts (if you're
offering them). 128Mb is definitely stingy for this, I'd recommend 256Mb
minimum for this. You might be okay with that amount of disk space
though, depending on how much accounting data you keep.

>3.Web Hosting (assuming 50,000 hits a day and 5GB of throughput)

A machine to itself, definitely ! Absolute minimum 256Mb, consider more.
Spread your pages across as many disks as you can to increase
throughput.

The 800 is a good little work-horse, but it's limited by internal
storage options, i.e. four disks at most and not many ISA/PCI slots.
Maxiumum RAM supported is 2Gb (in DIMM's), so don't skimp on it.

You might want to consider additional machines for handling your DNS -
these don't need to be so powerful though, say a couple of 300 series
machines for redundancy ?

If you're serious about protecting your machines you might want to
consider the HAFS (High Availability Firewall System) package, which is
an 800 series machine with Digital (Altavista) Firewall.

Hope this helps,
Chris
