> huge route tables over time.  Flags indicate routes are being learned
> via ICMP redirect.  Systems are not running any dynamic routing protocol
	Why aren't you running gated or routed ?

> (routed or gated).  Is there a way to prevent routes learned by ICMP
> redirect from being written into the routing table?

	Since your not running gated/routed the routing table is not
updated by them. However the routers on your network still have to
reply to the UNIX box to indicated where it should send its packets.
The UNIX box has to remember what it was told by ICMP so that the 
next packet that goes out has a route. Where else should the kernel
record this info but in the kernel table for routes.

> 
> Current workaround is to periodically flush the routes then reread the
> static routes back in, but I'd like a more elegant solution.

Just how big are they that you need to flush them? Why?

If you want to make sure they aren't large give them UNIX box some 
network routes so that it doesn't need to find every network/host by
first prinipals with arp and ICMP.

	What you describe would happen to any IP device!

