In article <8SkMrXA5OPI1Ewb7@oes-ee.demon.co.uk>, "Tim. Bluck." <tb@oes-ee.demon.co.uk> writes:
:        I have recently  aquired a Vax 11/750.

  Unless you are planning on using this VAX-11/750 as an auxillary
  electric heater for your house, as an auxillary radiator for your
  diesel-electric locomotive's regenerative braking system, or as a
  museum exhibit, may I ask why?  :-)

  The VAX-11/750 system is a very old, very slow (0.6 VUPS), very
  loud, and physically rather large VAX system, and it well predates
  most modern commonplace "niceties" -- such as SCSI and PCI.  The
  VAX-11/750 also requires multiple cabinets, minimal configurations
  require the base CPU cabinet and one expansion cabinet.  And by
  modern computing standards, the VAX-11/750 will tend to keep the
  local electrical service provider quite happy...  (But since you
  already seem to have L5-30R sockets wired up for this box...)

  There is a directly connected graphical display available with the
  VAXstation 100 configuration, but this configuration is now more of
  a trivia question than any sort of a real possibility -- VAX-11/750
  and VAX-11/780 systems with VAXstation 100 configurations were only
  rarely seen.  (Having bogged down all of a VAX-11/750 system by just
  moving the windows around...  :-)

  Most any VAX-11/750 system can easily be replaced by a much nicer and
  much more recent workstation systems, such as a VAXstation 3100 or
  VAXstation 4000 series workstation.

:                                                 After initial checks
:        I powered the system up,   I found that the   "reg-fail"  LED
:        on the power controller was lit,   I traced this over  to the
:        2.5V power sypply assembly   where I found that the   +/-5V &
:        +12V rails are not working. Has anyone had this fault before?
:        The power supply doesn't look to complicated.   But as I only 
:        have a block diagram, and not a full circuit for it I thought
:        I'd ask first..

  I do not know enough about the (mis)behaviour of the power controllers
  to answer this.

:        I'll  also be needing a boot tape for this system  as it only
:        has it's internal tape drive and no obvious way of connecting
:        to An  external drive of any kind.

  This "no obvious way" comment would tend to indicate you have no
  UNIBUS UDA50 series controller, and thus no way to connect to
  external RA-series disk drives -- the RA-series drives probably
  were the most common of all disk drives connected to the VAX-11/750.
  Various VAX-11/750 series systems also used Massbus controllers
  and drives -- these Massbus configurations were more rarely seen.

  The UDA50 bulkhead contains four rectangular Standard Disk
  Interconnect (SDI) connectors, each roughly thumb-sized, and each
  with a pair of screws.  The SDI connectors are arguably technically
  female connectors, but each will have an array of metalic male pins
  visible in the socket.  SDI cables are generally flat rectangular
  cables with silver metal connectors -- the cable is colored black.

  The UDA50 UNIBUS board set is a two-board set, and is known for
  having a set of quickly-flashing group of four LEDs on the card
  edge -- these four LEDs "strobe" in sequence when the UNIBUS is
  reset, and also tend to flicker while the UDA50 is idle.

  You will obviously see a cable connecting the UDA50 -- if a UDA50
  is present in the UNIBUS, obviously -- to the UDA50 bulkhead on
  the back of the VAX-11/750.

  If you provide the "M" numbers of the board(s) in the UNIBUS,
  we may/will be able to identify them for you.  The UDA50 should
  have the "M" numbers M7485 and M7486 on the edge of the card.
  M8728 and M8750 are the 256K and 1MB memory module "M" numbers.
  (The modules with "L" numbers are the CMI modules that comprise
  the core of the VAX-11/750 processor.)

:                                            Also the system only has
:        one serial port which I assume is for the console.  

  Correct.  When a terminal is connected and the system powered
  on, you should see "%%" to indicate microdiagnostics have passed,
  and then various other text from the processor...

:        Was this a standard configuration? One tape drive and One RS-
:        232 port would be fairly useless wouldn't it?

  This is the standard (base) configuration.  Systems with internal
  UNIBUS or UNIBUS expansion cabinets containing DMF32 and/or other
  similar UNIBUS serial line controllers are (were) quite common.

