For a few years we've been running an AlphaStation 600 5/266 with a Seagate
ST42100 disk on the SCSI bus without any problem.

However, now we have upgraded the machine to Digital UNIX 4.0d (from 3.2c)
we have been having read errors on the drive which the SCSI driver doesn't
seem to notice, but the higher levels of the kernel do (advfs and swapper).
It also only happens when the disk is being heavily accessed.

We are wondering if 4.0d is actually driving the interface faster, which is
causing the old disk not to be able to keep up, and so would like to
throttle down the speed of the SCSI bus, preferably on a per device basis.

Is this possible under Digital UNIX (I know it is for SunOS and Solaris
2.x)?

Does anyone have an alternative explanation/solution?

Stephen Usher
