In article <35212754.86CA517C@polygon.com>, "J. Buck Caldwell" <buck_c@polygon.com> writes:
:I'm the proud new papa of two UV3600's - which aren't so Micro - and that
:leads to the problem. I've got a MicroVAX II in a BA123 at home - can I put
:the CPU/MEM cards in the BA123, and plug in the bulkhead connecter currently
:on the BA123 into the KA650? And can a KDA50 and a RQDX3 co-exist? I'd
:really like to add the RA90 from the 3600 to my home machine...

  The BA2xx series Q-bus modules don't pass DMA grant in the BA123 and
  BA23, one would have to add a solder trace on the back of the module.

  (This and any other modifications to components or to the contents of
  the enclosures should, of course, only be done by folks trained and
  familiar with working within system enclosures, potentially near high
  voltages, and on potentially static-sensitive electronic componentry.
  If you are not one of these folks, then please do not -- equipment
  damage and/or personal injury and/or death can potentially result.)

  The KA650 and KA655 CPU and memory can operate in a BA123 and BA23
  enclosure, and the KA630 console bulkhead used in the BA123 and BA23
  series enclosures is fully compatible with the KA650 and KA655
  processor modules.

  You will want to make certain ancillary Q-bus modules in the BA123
  and BA23 are at current revision -- older revisions of the QDSS VCB02
  graphics controllers will work fine on the KA630, but correct operations
  with the KA650 and KA655 require an ECO.  The QVSS VCB01 controller is
  not supported as the console with the KA650 and KA655.

  And yes, one can configure two MSCP controllers, the CSR on one must
  be set as the primary/first controller, and as the secondary on the
  other controller.

:I wonder how long it would take to bring home a complete 3600 one carriable
:piece at a time...

  That task depends on a number of factors.

:Speaking of RA90, does anyone know what the fault code "E C6" means?
:Or was that "E 6C"... either would be helpful.

  If the RA90 fault problem persists, please contact your hardware
  service organization for assistance -- you'll need to provide the
  specifics of the particular error code(s) seen.  (The RA90 drive
  error codes C6 and 6C both potentially track back to fault(s) on
  the RA90 drive ECM first, but can then diverge to problem(s) with
  other drive componentry.)

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.ZZenet.dec.com
  note to those folks not contributing spam -- there is no ZZ in my address




